\par\par \b \uc2  Mexico's Fin Min: Opposed To Direct  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Invention \b0\par\par\uc2 595 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Februar 2003\par 18:25\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Mexico's Finance Minister Francisco Gil Diaz said Thursday his country remains fully committed to its free-floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime and dismissed the idea that authorities should tap the roughly $50 billion in international reserves to stem the recent slide in the peso. \par \par \uc2  "We think it's a waste of money," Gil Diaz told reporters in a press conference when asked about the possibility of direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  After strengthening against the dollar for several years, the peso ($1=MXN10.96) weakened more than 10% last year and has slipped further in 2003, reaching historical lows late last month. \par \par \uc2  Gil Diaz praised the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has tightened monetary policy to lower inflation expectations but refrained from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  He said \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  learned its lesson in 1995, when it unsuccessfully tried to halt the peso's depreciation after allowing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to float in late 1994. Gil Diaz is meeting with investors in New York City. \par \par \uc2  Gil Diaz said the Finance Ministry is sticking to its original 3% economic growth target for 2003, which would be about three times the expansion posted last year. \par \par \uc2  But he said the 2003 growth target is based on the assumption that there will be no U.S.-led war in Iraq, or if there is one, that it will be short. \par \par \uc2  "If it is a protracted type of event, it would certainly have a negative effect on Mexican growth," he told reporters. A possible U.S. invasion of Iraq is heightening fears of a double-dip recession in the U.S., which absorbs more than 80% of Mexican exports. \par \par \uc2  Gil Diaz said the government is bracing itself for tough global conditions by setting tight spending targets. The 2003 budget calls for a fiscal deficit ceiling equal to 0.5% of gross domestic product. \par \par \uc2  Mexico also completed its 2003 external financing needs in January, when it issued $2 billion in global bonds. \par \par \uc2  Gil Diaz said he met with the ratings agency Standard & Poor's earlier Thursday. He said S&P told him that of the 92 countries that face some fallout risk from a war in the Middle East, Mexico ranks as one of the less vulnerable. \par \par \uc2  Although Mexico has met its 2003 external financing needs, Gil Diaz didn't rule out that the country may return to foreign capital markets to retire its remaining Brady bonds by the end of 2003. \par \par \uc2  "It depends on the windows of opportunity we find," he told reporters. Mexico retired some more Brady bonds in January, and the amount of Bradys left in circulation today is "miniscule," he added. \par \par \uc2  Bradys are restructured sovereign bonds that were issued in the late 1980s and early 1990s and are backed by U.S. Treasury collateral. Over the past several years, Mexico has launched exchange offers in which the bonds are replaced with global bonds. \par \par \uc2  A side effect of the Iraqi war concern has been the surge in the price of oil, a major export commodity for Mexico. \par \par \uc2  Mexican oil currently fetches more than $25 a barrel, above the $18.30-a-barrel assumption contained in the government's 2003 budget. \par \par \uc2  Gil Diaz estimated that Mexico could ramp-up oil exports another 150,000-200,000 barrels a day in the second half of this year, if warranted by global demand. \par \par \uc2  Mexico exports around 1.76 million b/d of oil. \par \par \uc2  -By Mike Esterl, Dow Jones Newswires; 201 938-4026; mike.esterl@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030213dz2d0013t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
