\par\par \b \uc2  Mexico's Peso Opens At 10.8550/Dlr Vs 10.9450 Wed Close \b0\par\par\uc2 406 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Januar 2003\par 16:01\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  MEXICO CITY -(Dow Jones)- Mexico's peso opened sharply stronger against the dollar Thursday, buoyed by the dollar's  own rally against the euro. \par \par \uc2  In Mexico City, Banamex quoted the 48-hour spot peso opening at MXN10.8550 to the dollar, stronger from Wednesday's MXN10.9450 close. \par \par \uc2  A Mexico City trader said the peso's gains were tied to the dollar's gains against the euro. \par \par \uc2  The dollar rallied as banks took profits on weeks of euro gains, although the rebound isn't expected to be sustained with concerns over a war in Iraq still weighing on  the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The peso, which has racked up a string of new closing lows in the past week, has been hard hit by the dollar's weakness, given Mexico close trade and investment ties to the U.S. \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday, Bank of Mexico Governor Guillermo Ortiz attributed the peso's weakness largely to dollar losses against the euro, weaker-than-expected U.S. economic recovery, and Mexico's loss of competitiveness with other economies. \par \par \uc2  Ortiz reiterated that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't rule out use of any mechanisms at its disposal to counter volatility if peso weakness threatens inflation or inflation expectations, but stressed that the ultimate decision to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market lies with the Finance Ministry, which has the deciding vote in the exchange commission. \par \par \uc2  President Vicente Fox and Finance Minister Francisco Gil  both said at the weekend that \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  by selling dollars in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, but Ortiz rejected the notion of any rift between the bank and the government. \par \par \uc2  In the local media, his comments were taken by some to mean the bank is ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if necessary, and by others to mean that it doesn't intend to do so. \par \par \uc2  Bear Stearns, in a report Thursday entitled "Too Many Voices on Peso," said "the government and the Bank of Mexico are sending mixed messages about the peso, and confusion over policies tends to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in question." \par \par \uc2  Ortiz reaffirmed Wednesday that the peso's depreciation in the past 10 months has yet to show a clear impact on prices, but said the bank was watching closely for contamination of inflation via the exchange rate. \par \par \uc2  -By Anthony Harrup, Dow Jones Newswires; (5255) 5080-3450, anthony.harrup@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1027 1852 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030130dz1u00111\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
