\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 2-Mexico's cenbank says peso not spurring inflation. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Richard Jacobsen \par \uc2 520 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Januar 2003\par 22:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  MEXICO CITY, Jan 29 (Reuters) - The Mexican peso's swoon to record lows against the dollar has yet to fuel inflation that would force the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the bank's governor said on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Banco de Mexico Governor Guillermo Ortiz said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's mandate dictated that it only be concerned with the peso's volatility if it filters into the economy in the form of inflation, or expectations of future inflation. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not concerned about any exchange-rate level," Ortiz told a news conference on the bank's 2003 monetary program. "There is no magic number or magic level" for the peso. \par \par \uc2  The Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  closed at 10.972 to the dollar on Wednesday, its fourth consecutive record low closing against the greenback. Investors are concerned a war in Iraq could damage the U.S. economy, the destination for 90 percent of Mexican exports. \par \par \uc2  Banco de Mexico is closely watching the peso and will act with the "necessary force" if required to quell inflation pressures, Ortiz said. \par \par \uc2  But, he added, an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  "is not foreseeable under the conditions we're seeing in the markets these days." \par \par \uc2  Banco de Mexico's target for consumer price inflation in 2003 is 3 percent, far below 2002's inflation of 5.7 percent, and the bank has tightened monetary policy recently in its effort to meet this year's target. \par \par \uc2  Mexican prices rose at a moderate rate of 0.32 percent in the first half of January, while the peso shed 1.33 percent of its value against the dollar during the same period. \par \par \uc2  Banco de \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not directly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to calm volatility in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market since 1998. \par \par \uc2  Ortiz noted that any such \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has to be approved by a so-called exchange commission, on which the finance ministry's representative has veto power. Finance Minister Francisco Gil and President Vicente Fox said this week they have no intention of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the peso. \par \par \uc2  NERVOUS WORLD MARKETS \par \par \uc2  While declines in Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the past have often been a reflection of worsening domestic economic conditions, economists say the peso's recent weakness is more the result of nervousness across world markets about the possibility of a U.S.-led war against Iraq. \par \par \uc2  Still, economists caution, a prolonged U.S. war against Iraq would hurt the U.S. economy, in turn damaging the Mexican economy, which relies on the United Stats as its main export market and chief source of foreign direct investment. \par \par \uc2  The Mexican peso has weakened by more than 5 percent against the U.S. dollar since the end of 2002. It weakened 12 percent against the dollar last year. \par \par \uc2  In addition to the external factors, economists say some of the recent peso weakness can be attributed to investor concerns over stalled economic reforms in Mexico. Fox has failed to gain congressional support for new laws that would raise more taxes, lower electricity costs and make the local labor market more flexible. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030129dz1t011tm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{