\par\par \b \uc2  Mexico's Peso Ends At Another Low; Mkt Eyes  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2  By Anthony Harrup \par \uc2 563 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Januar 2003\par 21:24\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  MEXICO CITY -(Dow Jones)- Mexico's peso slipped against the dollar to its third new closing low in a week Monday, as market participants remained nervous over the brewing U.S.-Iraq conflict. \par \par \uc2  In Mexico City on 48-hour spot dollar markets, Banamex quoted the peso closing at a midrate of MXN10.9070 per dollar, weaker from its open of MXN10.8790 and from Friday's MXN10.8750 close. The closing spread was MXN10.8970/10.9170, compared with MXN10.86/10.89 Friday. \par \par \uc2  The continuing weakness of the peso, linked to the euro's gains against the dollar, has drawn differing views of how far the Bank of Mexico will let it go before taking action. \par \par \uc2  Bank of Mexico Governor Guillermo Ortiz said last week that the bank was keeping open all options to confront volatility and speculation hitting the peso, to the extent that it pushes up inflation expectations and threatens the bank's 3% inflation target for this year. \par \par \uc2  But Mexican President Vicente Fox said in Davos this weekend that the government will maintain its free float of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Our peso has handled this turbulence well and is in good conditions," Fox added. "Precisely because of that, we will continue the floating system, and we are not going to alter it." \par \par \uc2  Fox reiterated that Mexico has close to $50 billion in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Francisco Gil Diaz was also quoted in local media Monday as saying the government won't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to hold up the peso. \par \par \uc2  In a report Monday, HSBC welcomed the Bank of Mexico's decision Friday not to tighten monetary policy by increasing the size of its liquidity restriction, known as the "corto." \par \par \uc2  "Wisely, the Bank of Mexico left the corto unchanged, realizing that higher interest rates were unlikely to have any effect on protecting the beleaguered peso, " HSBC said, adding to "watch for Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell dollars" if the rate goes beyond past MXN10.95. \par \par \uc2  Guillermo Estebanez, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Bank of America, said he had mixed views about the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which would be the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's first since 1998. \par \par \uc2  Gil Diaz was against it, and the Finance Ministry has the deciding vote in decisions taken by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's exchange commission, he said. \par \par \uc2  While noting the government's reluctance to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Estebanez also cautioned that in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, things can get ugly fairly quickly. \par \par \uc2  The Bital bank noted the continued ties to euro/dollar moves Monday, and saw a short-term trading range between MXN10.70 and MXN11.00 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "We consider that under the current situation the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly in the market to contain the speculative rise in the exchange rate, which would be probably in case the rate went above MXN11.00," the bank said in its intraday report. \par \par \uc2  Bank of America's Estebanez said the peso will continue to move with the dollar against the euro, reflecting Mexico's close association with the U.S. economy. \par \par \uc2  "It's not something that \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets invented," he said. "If euro flows to the U.S. stop, they will also stop to Mexico." \par \par \uc2  -By Anthony Harrup, Dow Jones Newswires; (5255) 5080-3450, anthony.harrup@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1027 1852 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030127dz1r0019n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
