\par\par \b \uc2  DAVOS-UPDATE 1- \highlight22\uc2 Mexico \highlight\uc2  says will not  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\uc2  to support peso. \b0\par\par\uc2 286 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Januar 2003\par 14:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  DAVOS, Switzerland, Jan 26 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to support the weakening peso, preferring to allow markets to set the level, Finance Minister Francisco Gil Diaz said on Sunday. \par \par \uc2  "The policy is no \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  whatsoever. We are going to allow the market to work," Gil Diaz said on the sidelines of the World Economic Forum. \par \par \uc2  The Mexico peso has declined about 20 percent in value against the U.S. dollar in the past 10 months, Gil Diaz said. It fell again on Friday after the Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  held interest rates steady to close at 10.87 percent. Markets had anticipated a rate increase to offset the inflationary impact of a falling \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But Gil Diaz told Reuters he was confident that Mexico could achieve its inflation and growth targets of 3.0 percent this year despite the weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  He said the government's inflation report earlier this month showed price gains came in below 0.4 percent, which if sustained would mean it would undershoot its 3.0 percent inflation target for the year. \par \par \uc2  Moreover, Gil Diaz said, the potentially inflationary impact from a weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is lessening and the feed-through time lengthening, so he does not see the peso weakness as a threat. \par \par \uc2  "I am confident the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be able to achieve its inflation target," Gil Diaz said. \par \par \uc2  Economists, however, are doubtful. In a survey they forecast a 4.2 percent inflation rate for 2003. Last year Mexico missed its 4.5 percent target, posting consumer price increases of 5.7 percent. The Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  already has raised interest rates three times from September. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030127dz1q00cnz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
