\par\par \b \uc2  DAVOS- \highlight22\uc2 Mexico \highlight\uc2  says will not  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\uc2  to support peso. \b0\par\par\uc2 147 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Januar 2003\par 14:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  DAVOS, Switzerland, Jan 26 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to support the weakening peso, preferring to allow markets to set their own level, Mexico's Finance Minister Francisco Gil Diaz said on Sunday. \par \par \uc2  "The policy is no \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  whatsoever. We are going to allow the market to work," Gil Diaz said on the sidelines of the World Economic Forum. \par \par \uc2  The Mexico peso has declined about 20 percent in value against the U.S. dollar in the past 10 months, accelerating its fall on Friday after the Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  held interest rates steady. Markets had anticipated a rate increase to offset the inflationary impact of a falling \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But Gil Diaz told Reuters he was confident that Mexico could achieve its inflation and growth targets of 3.0 percent this year despite the weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030127dz1q00cny\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
