\par\par \b \uc2  Mexico shares, peso weaken on Wall St, dollar losses. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sam Dagher \par \uc2 525 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Juni 2002\par 16:54\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  MEXICO CITY, June 24 (Reuters) - Mexican stocks followed Wall Street lower on Monday, while the peso \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakened with the U.S. dollar with some market analysts predicting the Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to bolster the peso. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's benchmark IPC index fell 0.38 percent to 6,477.80. The index which lost 3.4 percent last week and 13 percent since mid-May is at its lowest level this year. \par \par \uc2  Among top losers were shares of America Movil, which is the most actively traded stock on the exchange, shedding 2.16 percent to 6.81 pesos per share, taking its loss to about 23 percent since the start of the year. \par \par \uc2  America Movil's New York-listed American Depositary Receipts (ADRs) fell 2.65 percent to $13.60. \par \par \uc2  In a report to clients after the market close on Friday Merrill Lynch said it is turning positive on the Mexican stock market citing attractive valuations following the recent sell-off in Mexican stocks and what it sees as strong economic growth prospects starting in the second half of 2002. \par \par \uc2  Merrill said historically low interest rates in Mexico and the convergence of the country's economy with that of the United States, its largest trading partner, would attract more funds into the equities market. \par \par \uc2  Merrill's senior country strategist Carlos Peyrelongue said his top picks were Mexico's largest bank BBVA Bancomer, media giant Televisa, the country's biggest retailer Wal-Mart de Mexico (Walmex) and brewer Grupo Modelo. \par \par \uc2  BBVA Bancomer, which is down 16 percent since early April, fell 1.09 percent to 8.15 pesos per share, while Walmex series "C" shares fell 0.52 percent to 22.90 pesos per share. \par \par \uc2  Televisa rose 0.21 percent to 19 pesos per share, \par \par \uc2  Fueling investor caution toward Mexican equities are concerns that falling U.S. equities and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets would force investors to trim their Mexican holdings to raise cash. \par \par \uc2  These same concerns have brought down the value of the Mexican peso \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the U.S. dollar by 9.25 percent since April 4. \par \par \uc2  In Monday trading thee peso shed 1.80 centavo from Friday to 9.9680 per U.S. dollar, while the dollar weakened to 0.98 per euro, its lowest level in two years. \par \par \uc2  Many market analysts predict the Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may restrict its daily credit injection in the money market to prevent the peso from falling through 10 per dollar and to calm inflation fears. \par \par \uc2  "It will be important to monitor inflationary expectations and results in the months ahead," said Michael Gavin, Latin American economist at UBS Warburg. "We think the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is ready to act if pass-through to prices becomes evident." \par \par \uc2  The Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is scheduled to release later on Monday inflation figures for the first half of June. \par \par \uc2  A Reuters survey of 31 private-sector economists predicted on average that consumer prices rose 0.15 percent during the period. The figures are scheduled to be released at 2:30 p.m. 1930 GMT), half an hour before the market close. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020624dy6o00tbp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
