\par\par \b \uc2  Mexico stocks down following Nasdaq, peso stable. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sam Dagher \par \uc2 521 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Juni 2002\par 17:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  MEXICO CITY, June 5 (Reuters) - Mexican stocks followed the U.S. Nasdaq lower early on Wednesday with blue-chip retailer Walmex, telephone giant Telmex and mining shares topping losers, while the peso was slightly weaker against the U.S. dollar, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Mexico's IPC index of 36 leading shares fell 0.65 percent to 6,970.83 points. \par \par \uc2  Topping the list of percentage losers were shares of Mexico's largest retailer Wal-Mart de Mexico (Walmex) , a favorite of foreign investors, falling 1.94 percent to 28.75 pesos. \par \par \uc2  Shares of Telefonos de Mexico (Telmex) , which account for about 16 percent of the IPC, fell 1.80 percent to 16.40 pesos. \par \par \uc2  Among other losers were shares of Mexican copper mining company Grupo Mexico , falling 2.29 percent to 16.20 pesos after the company said on Tuesday it shut down its Cananea copper mine in northern Mexico because it couldn't meet workers' wage demands. \par \par \uc2  In a report to clients on Wednesday, UBS Warburg said the closure of Cananea would reduce Grupo Mexico's cash flow and put it under pressure in servicing its heavy debt load. \par \par \uc2  "We maintain our cautious view and believe debt restructuring is key for Grupo Mexico, said Tom Meyer, analyst at UBS Warburg in New York. \par \par \uc2  Also weak were shares of Mexico's Industrias Penoles , the world's largest silver producer, falling 2.08 percent to 23.50 pesos. \par \par \uc2  Although analysts believe many Mexican stocks are attractive at current levels after last week's sell-off, investors are cautious and are looking for more evidence the Mexican economy - and that of its largest trading partner the United States - are on course for a recovery. \par \par \uc2  The IPC lost 4.5 percent last week and is down 6 percent since the start of May. \par \par \uc2  "We will continue to look to the United States for direction," said Jose Alberto Galvan, head of equities research at Finamex in Mexico City. \par \par \uc2  On Wall Street, stocks were mixed in late morning trade after an earlier report showed the U.S. services sector posted its fourth straight month of growth in May, expanding at its fastest pace since August 2000. The Dow Jones industrial average was last up 0.24 percent while the Nasdaq composite index was down 0.72 percent. \par \par \uc2  In the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, the peso weakened 0.60 centavo from Tuesday to 9.7390 per U.S. dollar after losing about 1 percent against the U.S. dollar on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "Continued peso volatility is likely to maintain pressure on short-term rates," said Deutsche Bank on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The peso has been hurt in recent days by the U.S. dollar's losses against the Japanese yen and euro. \par \par \uc2  The market's view that the peso will weaken further lifts interest rate expectations as investors believe \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the money market to restrict liquidity, which will boost the peso. \par \par \uc2  At Tuesday's Cetes auction of local Treasury bills, the rate on Mexico's benchmark 28-day Cetes rose seven basis points to 7 percent. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020605dy6500ukd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
