\par\par \b \uc2  Mexico exporters bemoan "super peso", tight policy. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Richard Jacobsen \par \uc2 697 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Juni 2001\par 22:32\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  MEXICO CITY, June 15 (Reuters) - Mexican business leaders have stepped up calls for the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to take action to weaken the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , saying the so-called "super peso" is threatening to kill off exporters. \par \par \uc2  But Banco de Mexico has thus far held steady amid the latest wave of public pressure from manufacturers and speculation in local markets. On Friday it dampened the speculation further by keeping its monetary policy unchanged in its daily money market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "It's time for Banco de \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  to loosen up its monetary policy, to support industry at this difficult time of economic slowdown," Claudio Gonzalez, the head of the Business Coordinating Council, told reporters on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Gonzalez spoke after business and banking leaders held a closed-door meeting with Banco de Mexico Governor Guillermo Ortiz and Finance Minister Francisco Gil to discuss the country's monetary and economic policies. \par \par \uc2  Mexico's export-oriented industry is in the midst of a sharp slowdown as the economy of the United States, which buys some 90 percent of Mexican exports, cools. Industrial production fell 3.1 percent in April after a 1.9 percent drop in March. \par \par \uc2  Exporters argue they are being made less competitive by the peso, which has strengthened about 6 percent against the dollar this year while other Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including Brazil's real and Chile's peso, are at new lows against the greenback. \par \par \uc2  A strong peso means companies' sales in dollars bring in fewer pesos in profits, unless the companies raise their prices in dollars abroad. However, despite the strong peso, Mexico's exports totaled $56 billion in the first four months of 2001, up 7.8 percent from the same 2000 period. \par \par \uc2  The benchmark 48-hour peso finished on Friday 6.3 centavos stronger at 9.012 pesos to the dollar, firming 0.5 percent in the week. \par \par \uc2  Economists say the chief reason for the peso's strength has been the heavy influx of foreign investment into Mexico, whose economy is viewed as a relatively safe haven compared to other emerging markets such as recession-plagued Argentina. \par \par \uc2  PESO BOGEYMAN \par \par \uc2  But manufacturing industry leaders have targeted Banco de Mexico as their main peso bogeyman. \par \par \uc2  They argue the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is sacrificing Mexican industry in its bid to drive inflation to 6.5 percent this year from 8.96 percent in 2000, and to U.S. levels of about 3.0 percent a year starting in 2003. \par \par \uc2  "What's clear is that we're not going to be around to enjoy the lower inflation," Rufino Salinas, head of the Glass Industry Association, said this week. He said many small-and medium-sized exporters will go out of business before the end of the year under current conditions. \par \par \uc2  Last month the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  eased its monetary policy for the first time after three years of tightening. It did so by reducing its so-called money-market "short" from 400 million pesos to 350 million pesos. \par \par \uc2  News of modest inflation in May - which saw the smallest rise in the May consumer price index since 1972 - followed by the weak industrial production data, fueled speculation this week Banco de Mexico would ease monetary policy anew. \par \par \uc2  Diminishing inflation expectations have dropped local interest rates to their lowest levels since 1994. Benchmark 28-day Cetes, or T-bills were auctioned at 9.51 percent on Tuesday, down almost nine percentage points from their 2001 peak in mid-January. \par \par \uc2  But Banco de Mexico appears to be unwilling to be too aggressive in loosening its monetary policy given lingering inflationary pressures in the economy, and particularly since consumer spending remains strong despite the production slowdown. \par \par \uc2  The Finance Ministry on Friday reported private consumption in the first quarter of 2001 rose 6.5 percent compared to the same 2000 period. \par \par \uc2  Economists, meanwhile, say Mexican businesses have granted wage increases this year that have outpaced productivity gains, a factor that has also reduced their competitiveness. \par \par \uc2  Unlike Banco de Mexico policy, "...this is a factor that is under the businessman's control," Jonathan Heath, chief economist at Latin Source consultants wrote in the Reforma daily. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010712dx6f01nj5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
