\par\par \b \uc2  CORRECTED - Mexico cenbank cuts money market short to 350 mln pesos. \b0\par\par\uc2 312 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Mai 2001\par 19:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  In MEXICO CITY story headlined "Mexico cenbank cuts money \par \par \uc2  market short to 350 mln pesos," in first paragraph please read \par \par \uc2  "... in the amount it withholds daily from the money market \par \par \uc2  ..." instead of "... of its daily injection of cash into the \par \par \uc2  money market." \par \par \uc2  In second paragraph, please read "... slashed the so-called \par \par \uc2  short ..." instead of "... slashed the amount of its daily \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  ..." \par \par \uc2  (Corrects references to injection and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) \par \par \uc2  A corrected version of the story follows: \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 MEXICO\b0 \highlight\uc2  CITY, May 18 (Reuters) - With Mexico's peso hovering at its strongest level since 1998, Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday cut the amount it withholds daily from the money market, its first cut in three years. \par \par \uc2  The Banco de Mexico said in its daily report that it slashed the so-called "short" to 350 million pesos ($38.8 million) from 400 million pesos, the first reduction since current restrictive monetary policy was put in place in March 1998. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's daily \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the local money market is its main mechanism for defending the value of the peso \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the dollar. The measure also holds local interest rates relatively high by keeping liquidity in the money markets lower than the market's estimated need. \par \par \uc2  Since 1998 the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been gradually raising the so-called short, or \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , tightening its monetary screws in a bid to combat inflation through relatively high interest rates aimed at keeping domestic consumption in check. \par \par \uc2  Mexico's interest rates have been ebbing in recent months, with the rate on the benchmark 28-day Cetes - or T-bill - government paper falling to a seven-year low at the government primary auction on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  For 2001, Mexico has set an official inflation goal of no higher than 6.5 percent, compared with 8.96 percent inflation in 2000. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010712dx5i03e2s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
