\par\par \b \uc2  Mexico Peso Closes Stronger At 9.3520/Dollar On `Short' \b0\par\par\uc2 280 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Juli 2000\par 21:00\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  MEXICO CITY -(Dow Jones)- Mexico's peso closed stronger against the dollar Monday after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  tightened monetary policy. \par \par \uc2  Banamex quoted the peso closing at a midrate of MXN9.3520 to the dollar on 48-hour spot dollar markets, stronger from its open of MXN9.3650 and from its close of MXN9.3800 Friday. Spreads at the close were MXN9.3470/9.3570 compared with MXN9.3650/9.3750 Friday. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Mexico raised its money-market "short" to MXN280 million from MXN230 million, signaling that it continues to worry about inflation. The bank said it found "various factors" that could impede its goal of lowering inflation to a rate similar to that of the U.S. and Canada by 2003. \par \par \uc2  The move came as no surprise, following \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Guillermo Ortiz's comments last week that additional monetary restrictions were likely. The short - which is the daily shortfall that the Bank of Mexico holds with commercial banks - was introduced as the bank's main monetary policy tool in March 1998. It has been raised 10 times since then, most recently on June 26. \par \par \uc2  "It's becoming less effective," Guillermo Estebanez, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of America in San Francisco. "The first time, it was taken seriously, but the 10th time...? This is a very marginal instrument in terms of monetary policy." \par \par \uc2  Expecations that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the money market heightened after the rate on \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's overnight Cetes, or Treasury bill, slid to 11.25% Friday. \par \par \uc2  -By Elliot Spagat, Dow Jones Newswires; (52-5) 726-9152; elliot.spagat@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1027 1852 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw7v03q41\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
