\par\par \b \uc2  Mexican peso strengthens at close. \b0\par\par\uc2 509 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Juni 2000\par 21:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  MEXICO CITY, June 28 (Reuters) - The Mexican peso rebounded on Wednesday to nearly erase the week's losses as the U.S. Federal Reserve met expectations, leaving interest rates alone. \par \par \uc2  The Fed decision, released 15 minutes before the close of peso trading in \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  City, came on the back of Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buoy the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ahead of the July 2 presidential election. \par \par \uc2  It also came as yields on Mexico's T-bills rose, helping to boost the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to recoup ground lost against the dollar in recent sessions, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "It's the combination of factors and a rebound from selling that's strengthened us here," said one trader. \par \par \uc2  The benchmark 48-hour peso rose 11.3 centavos to 9.9600/9.9650, trimming some of its early gains, but bouncing back after dropping a total of 13.3 centavos on Monday and Tuesday. The 24-hour contract gained 11.4 centavos to 9.9570/9.9620. \par \par \uc2  The same-day peso contract, used mostly by foreign investors to jump quickly in and out of the market, strengthened 11.2 centavos to 9.9500/9.9550. \par \par \uc2  "The raising of the 'short' and rising rates (on Mexican T-bills) are in some way taking a little pressure off the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Francisco Blanco, analyst at the Arka brokerage, referring to the Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday. \par \par \uc2  "Here, also, I believe that the statements from the finance ministry, as well as from Banco de Mexico, in the sense that they could be open to more tightening in monetary restrictions if the markets continue to show volatility, is a factor that contributed to making the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  better." \par \par \uc2  The peso has fluctuated throughout the month amid uncertainty about the presidential elections, viewed as the most hotly contested in recent Mexican history. Trading volume has been light, leaving the market vulnerable to exaggerated moves, traders said. \par \par \uc2  With Wednesday's gains, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is now down roughly 4.5 percent so far this month. As of Tuesday, it had dropped more than 5.6 percent from the start of June. \par \par \uc2  Economists, market analysts and fund managers say Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and economy should remain relatively stable whatever the outcome of the vote, which would represent a clear departure from recent history. \par \par \uc2  Since 1976, nearly every presidential succession has been accompanied by a peso crisis, the last being the 1994 devaluation at the start of President Ernesto Zedillo's term. \par \par \uc2  Before trading closed Wednesday, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  opted to leave interest rates unchanged, but signalled another increase could come in the future. The Fed has boosted rates six times since June 1999 in its campaign against inflation. \par \par \uc2  Higher U.S. rates are a bearish sign for emerging nations because companies find it harder to access credit abroad. \par \par \uc2  On Tuesday, primary rates on the Mexico's benchmark 28-day Cete, or T-bill, rose to 17.01 percent, its highest since Nov. 25, 1999, at a government auction. \par \par \uc2  ((-Kristin Roberts, Mexico City newsroom, +525 728 9549)). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010820dw6s02kpo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
