\par\par \b \uc2  Mexico's exports may rise 13 pct in 2000-Ortiz. \b0\par\par\uc2 464 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 April 2000\par 04:56\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  MEXICO CITY, April 10 (Reuters) - Mexico's exports could rise 13 percent this year compared with 1999 to reach $155 billion as the robust economy remains on its current path, said Banco de Mexico Governor Guillermo Ortiz on Monday. \par \par \uc2  "If we think that in 1994 the total exports of the country were $60 billion, we've seen an expansion with few precedents worldwide," said Ortiz, at a meeting of Mexican and Japanese executives. \par \par \uc2  In 1999, Mexico's exports were $136.7 billion. \par \par \uc2  In the first two months of the year, Mexico's exports grew at a 33.1 percent clip compared with the same 1999 period, while imports surged 31.6 percent. \par \par \uc2  While Mexico's trade is soaring, the country is still heavily dependent on the United States for an export market. In 1999, the Latin American nation sent $109.75 billion in goods and services to its northern neighbour - more than 88 percent of its total exports for the year. \par \par \uc2  Mexico's preliminary trade deficit for January and February was $889 million, close to the gap it reported in the first two months of last year. \par \par \uc2  "Despite the strong appreciation in the exchange rate, during 1999 and also in 2000, the exports continue to react with great vigour," Ortiz said. \par \par \uc2  Ortiz on Monday also repeated his firm denial that \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  is \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the country's exchange rate, repelling charges from presidential candidate Vicente Fox that economic authorities are manipulating the peso to keep it strong. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  does not manipulate the exchange rate," Ortiz told reporters after the executives' meeting. "It is the reduction in the country's credit risk that has fostered the peso's strengthening." \par \par \uc2  Mexico's credit rating was lifted to investment grade by Moody's last month, buoying markets and prompting the peso to climb to a 19-month high. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which rose 2.6 percent during the first quarter, has since slid and closed around 9.32 to the dollar on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Fox, the candidate for the conservative National Action Party (PAN), said last week the current administration might be allowing the peso to strengthen to convince the population that the economy is faring well ahead of July's presidential elections. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Jose Angel Gurria also responded to the charges from Fox, saying he backed the free-floating system now in play. \par \par \uc2  "We really have no problem of manipulation or \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the government in the markets, and on the other side, the free-floating regimen is a strength of the Mexican economy," Gurria told reporters. \par \par \uc2  Fox is running second in the polls ahead of the July 2 vote, trailing closely the ruling Institutional Revolutionary Party's candidate Francisco Labastida. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 2000. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw4b02l9d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
