\par\par \b \uc2  Mexico money market "short" won't change-Cenbank. \b0\par\par\uc2 202 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 April 2000\par 20:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  MEXICO CITY, April 5 (Reuters) - Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Wednesday it has no plans to change the amount of cash by which it keeps the money market short each day, quelling speculation that it might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the peso. \par \par \uc2  "The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not contemplating measures related to the 'short,'" said Armando Baqueiro, head of economic studies at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Mexico drains the money market by 180 million pesos ($19.2 million) daily to keep liquidity tight, part of an effort to fight inflation. It last changed the policy in January, when it raised the amount from 160 million pesos. \par \par \uc2  In recent days, markets have speculated that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might step in to weaken the peso. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit a 19-month peak of around 9.2 to the dollar in late March on optimism over the strong economy and an upgrade of the nation's credit rating by firms Moody's and Standard & Poor's. \par \par \uc2  The 48-hour peso slipped in Wednesday's trading to 9.3950 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  (Mexico City newsroom, +525 728-7902, mexicocity.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 2000. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw4502xzd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
