\par\par \b \uc2  Mexico short-end T-bills pressured by Brazil fears. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Rosario Torres Limon \par \uc2 368 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1999\par 06:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  MEXICO CITY, March 2 (Reuters) - Renewed jitters over Brazil's sinking real \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pushed short-end Mexican Cetes or T-bill rates slightly higher at Tuesday's weekly primary auction, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Short-term rates also rose slightly because the market felt it was time to level off after five consecutive weeks of declines, economic analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "The resurgence of external pressures, like the greater depreciation of the real and its impact on the (Mexican) peso, caused the slight rise in the 27-day Cetes," said Martin Delgado, analyst at Value brokerage in Mexico City. \par \par \uc2  The bellwether 28-day Cetes - a 27-day paper at Tuesday's auction - rose seven basis points to 26.78 percent compared with the previous week. \par \par \uc2  Other maturities continued to decline on favourable longer term inflation prospects, with the 91-day Cetes sinking three basis points to 27.04 percent, the 161-day Cetes down 34 basis points to 26.52 percent and the 343-day paper five basis points lower at 26.96 percent. \par \par \uc2  Carlos Samano, director of analysis at Bancomer brokerage, said the "marginal increase in the short-term rate was because the time had come to find a floor after five auctions with consecutive declines." \par \par \uc2  "It's not an important rebound; it's just an adjustment," he added. \par \par \uc2  The Brazilian real, allowed to float in January after overwhelming pressure from markets, slipped to a new low of 2.17 per dollar on Tuesday, forcing the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peso was dragged down a notch too, with the benchmark 48-hour peso contract shedding 1 centavo to end at 9.945/9.95 per dollar after paring heavier earlier losses. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said primary Cetes rates on Tuesday were also affected by a sharp 38.8 percent fall in demand to 14.22 billion pesos, compared with demand the week before. \par \par \uc2  "Demand fell for all maturities because investors preferred to stay on the sidelines with the uncertainty over external factors," said Mireya Gomez, an analyst at Bursametrica Management. \par \par \uc2  ((Mexico City newsroom +525 728-7902, mexicocity.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv3303dwg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
