\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS - Mexico peso crawls back from abyss. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Richard Jacobsen \par \uc2 652 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Oktober 1998\par 00:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  MEXICO CITY, Oct 22 (Reuters) - The Mexican peso has crawled back from its mid-September abyss, taking advantage of recent calm in international financial markets to stand under 10 to the dollar on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  But analysts said they did not expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to advance much further in coming weeks as concerns over Latin America's economic giant Brazil and the Mexican Congress's budget debate keep investors timid. \par \par \uc2  On Thursday the benchmark 48-hour peso contract closed below the 10-to-the-dollar level for the first time since September 2, ending the day at 9.96/9.97 per dollar. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has recovered about 7.6 percent of its value since tumbling to a record low of 10.73 to the dollar on September 10, when investors fled Latin American markets amid fears Brazil would be the next emerging economy to succumb to an Asian-style meltdown. \par \par \uc2  Mexico's free-floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  made the peso especially vulnerable to the fear and loathing that gripped Latin American markets in the late summer. \par \par \uc2  "I think now we're unwinding that part of the move that was not justifiable by (Mexico's) economic fundamentals," said James Barrineau, Latin America strategist at Salomon Smith Barney. \par \par \uc2  The peso on Thursday still stood down about 19 percent from its 1998 starting point of 8.065 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  This year's declining oil revenues and increased inflation expectations meant the peso had reason to cede some ground to the dollar, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  But Mexican monetary officials took steps to limit what they saw as an unwarranted run on the money as Latin American financial markets appeared on the verge of unravelling. \par \par \uc2  As the peso hit all-time lows in early September, Banco de Mexico tightened its monetary screws a fifth time this year by raising the amount it leaves the local money market short each day to 100 million pesos. The prior daily short was 70 million pesos. \par \par \uc2  There was talk in Mexican markets on Thursday that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may soon loosen monetary policy, a move that could lower local interest rates and take some pressure off the economy. \par \par \uc2  In the event Banco de \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not alter the short when it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the money market on Thursday, however it may still review its policy after weighing Thursday's release of first-half October inflation data, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Banco de Mexico said the consumer price index rose 0.78 percent in the first two weeks of October, in line with analysts' forecasts. \par \par \uc2  But the forthcoming debate on the 1999 budget, which President Ernesto Zedillo's government has to present to Congress by November 15, will keep a lid on further gains in the peso. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said they expected a budget deal would be reached, but not after public wrangling that will make for nervous markets. \par \par \uc2  "We think there could be a little more volatility because of the debate over the budget, (bank-bailout fund) Fobaproa and the series of decisions that have to be made between now and the end of the year," said Alfredo Thorne, head of economic research at J.P. Morgan's Mexico \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But he said he expected the peso to end the year at 9.8 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Investor expectations that Brazil will adopt fiscal measures paving the way for securing multibillion dollar loans have been key to the peso's recent recovery. \par \par \uc2  But if Brazil were to disappoint markets or if the U.S. economy falters despite recent Federal Reserve interest-rate cuts, the peso will come under renewed fire, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "I'm optimistic (about the peso's outlook) but there is still some room for disappointment," said Javier Maldonado, chief economist for Latin America at Bank of Montreal. \par \par \uc2  ((Mexico City newsroom +525 728 9559 mexicocity.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021113duam02gd6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
