\par\par \b \uc2  Mexican peso closes at historic low on uncertainty. \b0\par\par\uc2 291 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 September 1998\par 21:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  MEXICO CITY, Sept 11 (Reuters) - The Mexican peso closed at a historic low for the third day in a row on Friday as continued uncertainty on world financial markets encouraged investors to buy up dollar positions, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The benchmark 48-hour peso lost 7.0 centavos to close at 10.58/10.62 per dollar despite Thursday's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  aimed at shoring up the ailing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Banco de \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought nearly half a billion dollars worth of pesos and tightened monetary policy on Thursday, when global market turbulence sent Mexican stock prices to two-and-a-half year lows and weakened the peso to a historic intraday low of 10.72 pesos per dollar. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Mexican stocks bounced back on Friday as the IPC index\tab   was up 1.5 percent in late trade, but dealers said that wasn't enough.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The recovery of share prices calmed the market, but there's still a lot of nervousness," one \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trader said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Stock rebounded in Brazil\tab    and on Wall Street\tab   also helped moderate further peso losses, traders said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But because \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Gov. Guillermo Ortiz declared on Thursday that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Mexico\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was prepared to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market whenever it saw fit, many investors and companies went short rather than long on the dollar to guard against a peso recovery, dealers said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "They're covering only for 48 hours and right now nobody wants to buy excessive (dollar) positions because it could turn out to be very expensive if Banco de \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Mexico intervenes\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," one trader said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The 24-hour peso lost 5.0 centavos to 10.57/10.60.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ((Elena Vega, Mexico City newsroom, +525 728-9554)).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 1998.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021108du9b00wyt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
