\par\par \b \uc2  Mexican Peso Opens -2: Mkt Watching Brazil, Clinton, Stks \b0\par\par\uc2 337 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 September 1998\par 16:13\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  MEXICO CITY (Dow Jones)--Mexico's peso opened stronger against the dollar Friday amid continued nervousness as participants kept watch on Brazil, President Bill Clinton and the stock markets. \par \par \uc2  In Mexico City around 1405 GMT, on 48-hour spot dollar markets, banking leader Banamex quoted the peso trading at a midrate of 10.5450 per dollar against 10.6000 per dollar at Thursday's close. Spreads were 10.5300/10.5600 pesos per dollar, compared with 10.5500/10.6500 Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Banamex quoted the spot peso opening at a midrate of 10.5650 per dollar on a spread of 10.5400/10.5900. \par \par \uc2  Other banks had the spot dollar trading at midrates ranging from 10.5250 to 10.5800. \par \par \uc2  "The market is still very volatile. The rate is below the opening rate, but there is a lot of nervousness," said a Mexico City trader. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader said participants were keeping a close eye on Brazil, on the problems of President Bill Clinton and on stock markets. \par \par \uc2  The trader said the market still lacked liquidity, and that \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could continue although there was no evidence of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in early trading. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold $478 million Thursday to contain the slide of the peso, including $200 million in automatic auctions that take place when the spot rate moves 2% from the previous day's fix. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Guillermo Ortiz said at a news conference late Thursday that the bank will continue to take the measures it considers necessary to confront the current volatility. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Mexico also tightened further its daily lending restriction with commercial banks Thursday, which pushed interest rates up further. \par \par \uc2  The Dow Jones Industrial Average was gaining 1.5% while Mexican stocks were up 2.8%, reflecting the rebound in Brazil's Bovespa index. The IPC fell 9.8% Thursday. \par \par \uc2  -By Anthony Harrup (525) 726-9152; aharrup@ap.org \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 11-09-98 \par \par \uc2  1413GMT \par \par \uc2  1027 1852 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du9b07udm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
