\par\par \b \uc2  WRAP: Mexico Signals More Active Role To Bolster Peso \b0\par\par\uc2  By Elliot Spagat \par \uc2 285 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 September 1998\par 06:43\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  MEXICO CITY (Dow Jones)--As recently as three months ago, President Ernesto Zedillo said Mexico shouldn't spend "a nickel" of international reserves to fight speculation. That was before turmoil in global markets sent the peso into a tailspin. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market for the first time since December 1995, apart from the $200 million auctions it holds when the peso weakens 2.0% against the dollar from the previous day's fix. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it sold $278 million, in addition to the $200 million auction. \par \par \uc2  Economists predicted the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would spend more reserves - and guide interest rates higher - if the market continued to deteriorate, marking another departure from its passive approach to the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market since the government floated the peso in December 1994. \par \par \uc2  Bank of Mexico Governor Guillermo Ortiz and Finance Minister Jose Angel Gurria insisted they weren't defending a specific exchange rate and only sought to inject liquidity into an unusual, disorderly market. \par \par \uc2  "By not having to defend a determined exchange rate, we're not subject to a constant, unforseen bleeding of international reserves," Ortiz told a news conference. "What's fundamental is to give the market liquidity in these difficult circumstances and try to reduce volatility to the extent we can." \par \par \uc2  The peso has declined 16% against the dollar since the beginning of August and hit a sixth straight all-time closing low Thursday, ending at a mid-rate of 10.6000 to the dollar. The Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fluctuated wildly between 10.3200 and 10.7300 during the session. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 11-09-98 \par \par \uc2  0443GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du9b07u8q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
