\par\par \b \uc2  CORRECTED - ANALYSIS-Tight money not seen saving Mexico peso. \b0\par\par\uc2 524 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 September 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  In MEXICO CITY story headlined "ANALYSIS-Tighter money not seen saving Mexico peso" please read in second paragraph ...dollar sales by Banco de Mexico of a total $478 million in two automatically triggered auctions and one "discretionary" sale... instead of ...dollar sales by Banco de Mexico of a total $378 million in one automatic auction and one "discretionary" sale...(corrects dollar amount and number of \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 ). \par \par \uc2  A corrected version follows. \par \par \uc2  By Rosario Torres Limon \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 MEXICO\b0 \highlight\uc2  CITY, Sept 10 (Reuters) - Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday sold a heap of dollars and moved for the fifth time this year to tighten monetary policy, but analysts said the measures were unlikely to save the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from global turmoil. \par \par \uc2  The benchmark 48-hour peso hit an intraday low of 10.73 per dollar, but dollar sales by Banco de Mexico of a total $478 million in two automatically triggered auctions and one "discretionary" sale helped lift the peso to a close of 10.52/10.55 per greenback. \par \par \uc2  Banco de Mexico also announced it was increasing the amount it leaves the money markets short by every day to 100 million pesos from 70 million pesos previously. \par \par \uc2  Traders said a meltdown in Brazilian shares, which closed 15.82 percent down amid heavy dollar outflows over devaluation fears, and jitters over the fate of "Sexgate"-plagued U.S. President Bill Clinton, hammered market confidence. \par \par \uc2  "Banco de Mexico is trying to contain the pressures, but the truth is they are so big and of such an international nature that they are beyond the bank's influence," said the director of analysis at Bancomer brokerage, Carlos Samano. \par \par \uc2  With Thursday's plunge, the free-floating Mexican peso has depreciated by 23.5 percent against the dollar this year. \par \par \uc2  Despite the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves, analysts said they saw further downside for the peso as investors continued to desert Brazil, Russia staggered on in indecision and the U.S. Congress pondered a possible impeachment of Clinton for allegedly trying to cover up a sexual relationship with a White House intern. \par \par \uc2  "The short-term perspective remains pessimistic due to volatility, uncertainty over Brazil, Russia and the Clinton situation," said Hector Velez, analyst at Cargill de Mexico. \par \par \uc2  "The great risk we face is that Brazil won't hold out. The outflows of capital from Brazil in recent days have been high," Velez added. \par \par \uc2  Traders said Brazil lost more than $2.0 billion on Thursday. Earlier in the day, the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said its foreign reserves had slipped around $12 billion so far in September to about $55 billion. \par \par \uc2  "I find it hard to believe the peso will recover until markets stabilise," said Sergio Garcia, director of analysis at the Value brokerage. \par \par \uc2  Analysts noted that benchmark Cetes or T-bill rates reached 40 percent during the day, but this failed to brake the slide in the peso. \par \par \uc2  "There are more negative factors than positive factors and this could translate into more volatility and an even weaker exchange rate," said Velez, \par \par \uc2  ((Mexico City newsroom +525 728-7902, mexicocity.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021108du9a00u3c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
