\par\par \b \uc2  CORRECTED - Mexican stocks conclude rocky session down 9.8 pct. \b0\par\par\uc2 568 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 September 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  In MEXICO CITY story headlined "Mexican stocks conclude rocky session down 9.8 pct" please read in sixth paragraph .... forcing the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to inject dollars into the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market three times for a total of $478 million .... instead of .... forcing the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to twice inject dollars into the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market (correcting number of \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  and adding dollar total). \par \par \uc2  A corrected version follows. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 MEXICO\b0 \highlight\uc2  CITY, Sept 10 (Reuters) - Mexican stocks plunged to two-and-a-half-year lows on Thursday amid a hemispheric sell-off triggered by capital flight in Brazil and political fallout in the United States due to the investigation of President Bill Clinton. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The IPC index of 35 leading stocks\tab   tumbled 311.16 points or 9.8 percent to 2856.10 points.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par It was the largest fall in point and percentage terms since Oct. 27, 1997, when Asia's economic crisis first took a bite out of stock markets everywhere. It also was the lowest closing level since March 15, 1996.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "What's happening is a little more noise coming from Brazil ... as well as the United States, where there is starting to be some concern that Clinton may have to resign," Sergio Garcia, director of analysis at the Value brokerage, told Reuters.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par One desk trader was more blunt: "We can blame Brazil for dragging us down and also Clinton, who made the markets nervous when they least needed it."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Stocks sank as the peso hit a historic low versus the dollar, forcing the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to inject dollars into the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market three times for a total of $478 million and to tighten monetary policy in defence of the peso.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In Brazil, the Bovespa\tab    sank 15.82 percent, while the Dow Industrials closed 3.17 percent lower.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Investors sought refuge in safer, low-yielding securities. The 30-year U.S. Treasury bond rose 32/32, lowering the yield to 5.20 percent.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Mexican slide was felt across the board. Of 90 active issues, only two gained while 85 lost and three went unchanged. Volume was heavy at 138.8 million shares.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ((Elena Vega, Mexico City newsroom, +525 728-9554, mexicocity.newsroom@reuters.com)).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "What's happening is a little more noise coming from Brazil ... as well as the United States, where there is starting to be some concern that Clinton may have to resign," Sergio Garcia, director of analysis at the Value brokerage, told Reuters.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par One desk trader was more blunt: "We can blame Brazil for dragging us down and also Clinton, who made the markets nervous when they least needed it."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Stocks sank as the peso hit a historic low versus the dollar, forcing the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to twice inject dollars into the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market and to tighten monetary policy in defence of the peso.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In Brazil, the Bovespa\tab    sank 14.68 percent, while the Dow Industrials closed 3.17 percent lower.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Investors sought refuge in safer, low-yielding securities. The 30-year U.S. Treasury bond rose 33/32, lowering the yield to 5.20 percent.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Mexican slide was felt across the board. Of 90 active issues, only two gained while 85 lost and three went unchanged. Volume was heavy at 138.8 million shares.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ((Elena Vega, Mexico City newsroom, +525 728-9554, mexicocity.newsroom@reuters.com)).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 1998.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021108du9a00ty6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
