\par\par \b \uc2  CORRECTED - Mexican stocks tumble to 2-1/2 year low. \b0\par\par\uc2 346 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 September 1998\par 01:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2    In MEXICO CITY story headlined "Mexican stocks tumble to 2-1/2 year \par \par \uc2    low" please read in sixth paragraph .... "forcing the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \par \par \uc2    inject dollars into the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market three times for a total \par \par \uc2    of $478 million" .... instead of .... "forcing the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \par \par \uc2    twice inject dollars into the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market (correcting \par \par \uc2    number of \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  and adding dollar total). \par \par \uc2  A corrected version follows. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 MEXICO\b0 \highlight\uc2  CITY, Sept 10 (Reuters) - Mexican stocks plunged to 2-1/2 year lows on Thursday amid a hemispheric sell-off triggered by capital flight in Brazil and political fallout in the United States due to the investigation of President Bill Clinton. \par \par \uc2  The IPC index of 35 leading stocks tumbled 311.16 points, or 9.8 percent, to 2,856.10 points. \par \par \uc2  It was the largest fall in point and percentage terms since Oct. 27, 1997, when Asia's economic crisis first took a bite out of stock markets everywhere. It also was the lowest closing level since March 15, 1996. \par \par \uc2  "What's happening is a little more noise coming from Brazil ... as well as the United States, where there is starting to be some concern that Clinton may have to resign," Sergio Garcia, director of analysis at the Value brokerage, told Reuters. \par \par \uc2  One desk trader was more blunt: "We can blame Brazil for dragging us down and also Clinton, who made the markets nervous when they least needed it." \par \par \uc2  Stocks sank as the peso hit a historic low versus the dollar, forcing the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to inject dollars into the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market three times for a total of $478 million and to tighten monetary policy in defence of the peso. \par \par \uc2  The slide was felt across the board. Of 90 active issues, only two gained while 85 lost and three were unchanged. Volume was heavy at 138.8 million shares. \par \par \uc2  In Brazil, the Bovespa sank 15.82 percent, while the Dow Industrials closed 3.17 percent lower. \par \par \uc2  Investors sought refuge in safer, low-yielding securities. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021108du9a00s9o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
