\par\par \b \uc2  Mexican Cetes rates soar on tight market liquidity. \b0\par\par\uc2 229 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Juli 1998\par 00:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  MEXICO CITY, July 13 (Reuters) - Mexican overnight Cetes rates closed 175 basis points higher at 21.50 percent on Monday. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Dealers said that secondary money market\tab    rates increased due to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  draining liquidity and large firms deliberately withholding cash from the system.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  "Rates came under pressure at the close from lack of market liquidity, due in part to Banco de \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and speculation by big players," a trader said. \par \par \uc2  Banco de Mexico drained 762 million pesos by auctioning four direct Cetes sales and one repurchase agreement. \par \par \uc2  Government paper rates dipped as low as 19.45 percent in early trade when the Mexican peso advanced on positive emerging market reaction to an international aid package for Russia. \par \par \uc2  Benchmark 48-hour peso contracts closed 3.7 centavos stronger at 8.8925/8.8950 per dollar. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  also advanced because the yen withstood the resignation of Japanese Prime Minister Ryutaro Hashimoto after his Liberal Democratic Party lost seats in Upper House elections on Sunday. \par \par \uc2  Overnight rates on bank paper, meanwhile, rose 10 basis points to 21.50 percent. \par \par \uc2  Liquidity of 490 million pesos was due to flow out of the market on Tuesday from prior \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  credits falling due. \par \par \uc2  ((Julia Denegre, Mexico City newsroom +525 728-7916, mexicocity.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba000002001092