\par\par \b \uc2  US attache's weekly "Hot Bites from Mexico" report. \b0\par\par\uc2 2,660 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Juni 1998\par 21:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2     WASHINGTON, June 17 (Reuters) - The following is a weekly "Hot Bites from \par \par \uc2  Mexico" report by the agricultural attache at the U.S. embassy in Mexico City. \par \par \uc2      The report, dated May 28, was released Wednesday by the U.S. Agriculture \par \par \uc2  Department. \par \par \uc2      Attache reports are not official USDA data. \par \par \uc2  Welcome to Hot Bites from Mexico, a weekly review of issues of \par \par \uc2  interest to the U.S. agricultural community. The topics covered \par \par \uc2  in this report reflect developments in Mexico that have been \par \par \uc2  garnered during travel around the country, reported in the media, \par \par \uc2  or offered by host country officials and agricultural analysts. \par \par \uc2  Readers should understand that press articles are included in \par \par \uc2  this report to provide insights into the Mexican "mood" facing \par \par \uc2  U.S. agricultural exporters. Significant issues will be expanded \par \par \uc2  upon in subsequent reports from this office. \par \par \uc2  DRY WEATHER CONTINUES \par \par \uc2  - \par \par \uc2  Mexico's annual rainfall level through April 1998 was 52.3 \par \par \uc2  percent less than last year and 41 percent less than the annual \par \par \uc2  average. According to the press, Mexico experienced the driest \par \par \uc2  April and May on record. The GOM says the lack of normal \par \par \uc2  precipitation is expected to reduce overall agricultural \par \par \uc2  production, but has stopped short of declaring a disaster. The \par \par \uc2  lack of normal rainfall is said to have reduced reservoir levels \par \par \uc2  throughout the country, especially in the northwest where they \par \par \uc2  are at a reported 19.3 percent of capacity compared to the \par \par \uc2  average for this time of year of 29.3 percent. Reservoirs in the \par \par \uc2  state of Sinaloa are reportedly among the lowest at only 2.9 \par \par \uc2  percent of capacity. Although most of Mexico is experiencing \par \par \uc2  drier than normal conditions, two states - Baja California Sur \par \par \uc2  (in the north) and Quintana Roo (in the south) - have \par \par \uc2  experienced above normal rainfall. (Sources El Economista \par \par \uc2  5/26/98, l Financiero 5/27/1998) \par \par \uc2  FAS/MEXICO COMMENT: The current El Nino-driven dryness continues \par \par \uc2  to plague Mexico. Although 1998/99 grain production is forecast \par \par \uc2  to be 5 percent above last season, rainfall is needed within the \par \par \uc2  next few weeks to permit planting the grain crops. The outlook \par \par \uc2  for the coarse grain crop remains dependent upon rainfall \par \par \uc2  arriving in late May to early June. Meanwhile, poor pasture \par \par \uc2  conditions and water shortages in the north have caused \par \par \uc2  "distress" sales of cattle. \par \par \uc2  DUMPING CLAIMS BY MEXICO'S BEEF AND PORK INDUSTRIES \par \par \uc2  - \par \par \uc2  Mexico's beef and pork industries are said to be increasingly \par \par \uc2  close to filing separate antidumping petitions against U.S. \par \par \uc2  growers, processors and exporters. Speaking for Mexico's beef \par \par \uc2  industry, leaders of the National Cattlemen's confederation (CNG) \par \par \uc2  recently said that, while they don't necessarily blame U.S. \par \par \uc2  cattlemen, imports of U.S. beef have soared in 1997 and 1998 \par \par \uc2  relative to 1996 levels, when a similar antidumping claim was \par \par \uc2  settled with a bilateral agreement between the two countries' \par \par \uc2  cattle groups. They also blame the Mexican government for \par \par \uc2  allowing in "low quality" beef and "old, sick cattle" which has \par \par \uc2  reduced live weight prices to about 8.50 pesos/kg (US$0.45/lb) \par \par \uc2  for Mexican cattle. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, Mexico's pork industry is now said to be close to \par \par \uc2  filing its own petition against U.S. pork.  The president of the \par \par \uc2  Mexican Pork Council (CMP) is quoted as saying "We aren't \par \par \uc2  bothered by the volumes imported as long as they are not priced \par \par \uc2  below the cost of production, known as dumping".  The CMP alleges \par \par \uc2  that since October 1997, falling U.S. pork prices have caused \par \par \uc2  Mexican pork prices to drop from 12 pesos to 9.50 pesos per kilo \par \par \uc2  (currently US$1.41 to 1.12).  The CMP's antidumping petition has \par \par \uc2  reportedly already been prepared, asking for a halt to Mexico's \par \par \uc2  favourable in-quota TRQ duties under NAFTA.  At the same time, \par \par \uc2  however, both the cattlemen and hog growers admit that their \par \par \uc2  biggest problem is that they are uncompetitive due to much higher \par \par \uc2  feed and credit costs.  (Sources: Reforma, 5/25/98; El \par \par \uc2  Financiero, 5/26/98) \par \par \uc2  MEXICAN COFFEE PRODUCTION AT RISK FOR LACK OF WATER \par \par \uc2  ---------------------------------------------------- \par \par \uc2  According to the Mexican Confederation of Coffee Producers (CMPC) \par \par \uc2  certain coffee production areas in the states of Chiapas, Oaxaca \par \par \uc2  and Puebla are at risk of losing coffee trees due to the severe \par \par \uc2  dryness and lack of normal rainfall.  CMPC estimates that if \par \par \uc2  rains are delayed beyond the next two weeks, production could be \par \par \uc2  reduced by 500,000 60-kg bags for 1998/99.  The seriousness of \par \par \uc2  this situation is not the immediate reduction in production, but \par \par \uc2  the long term effects this phenomenon will have on small coffee \par \par \uc2  producers who make a marginal living from coffee.  New coffee \par \par \uc2  plants take from 3 to 4 years before they produce, and many small \par \par \uc2  producers are concerned because they have no other means for \par \par \uc2  their livelihood but coffee production.  (Source:  Reforma \par \par \uc2  5/27/98) \par \par \uc2  FAS/MEXICO COMMENT:  Although extreme dryness, fires and lack of \par \par \uc2  rainfall will undoutably affect coffee production in some areas, \par \par \uc2  it is still too early to quantify.  Therefore, we will maintain \par \par \uc2  the current production estimate of 5.55 million 60-kg bags for MY \par \par \uc2  1998/99.  Furthermore, some sources indicate that coffee \par \par \uc2  producers and exporters are somewhat between "a rock and a hard \par \par \uc2  place".  Producers and exporters are likely to "cry wolf" that \par \par \uc2  production will be limited in order to obtain better prices on \par \par \uc2  the international market and, on the other hand, producers have \par \par \uc2  to convince the Mexican authorities and coffee processors that \par \par \uc2  there is sufficient supply to cover domestic demand, thus \par \par \uc2  avoiding imports of coffee beans like last year.  For further \par \par \uc2  coffee information, refer to our annual coffee report MX8043 \par \par \uc2  dated 5/15/98. \par \par \uc2  ATO EXPHOTEL PRESS CONFERENCE \par \par \uc2  ----------------------------- \par \par \uc2  On Thursday, May 28, the Director of the Agricultural Trade \par \par \uc2  Office (ATO) hosted a meeting with key members of the Cancun \par \par \uc2  press to present details on the first ATO-sponsored U.S. Pavilion \par \par \uc2  at the second annual EXPHOTEL trade show in Cancun on June 10-12. \par \par \uc2  The focus of EXPHOTEL is on top quality food, products and \par \par \uc2  equipment used by the hotel, restaurant, industrial cafeteria and \par \par \uc2  hospitality (HRI) industries. The U.S. Pavilion includes 25 U.S. \par \par \uc2  private companies and trade organisations which will feature an \par \par \uc2  impressive selection of value-added U.S. food and agricultural \par \par \uc2  products, including U.S. poultry, meats and speciality products, \par \par \uc2  peanuts, fresh and processed fruits and vegetables, juices, dairy \par \par \uc2  products and grocery products. At EXPHOTEL 97, 109 Mexican and \par \par \uc2  international companies exhibited their products to over 3,000 \par \par \uc2  decision makers in the food service industry. Based on the \par \par \uc2  success of last year's show, EXPHOTEL 98 will feature twice as \par \par \uc2  many exhibitors. For more details on the U.S. Pavilion, contact \par \par \uc2  Lourdes Guzman at the ATO at TEL: 011-525-202-0168; FAX: \par \par \uc2  011-525-202-0528. (Source: ATO/Mexico) \par \par \uc2  ATO BULLISH ON MEXICAN MARKET \par \par \uc2  - \par \par \uc2  The Agricultural Trade Office (ATO) in Mexico City is preparing \par \par \uc2  to launch an aggressive campaign through outreach efforts to \par \par \uc2  various U.S. companies and trade organisations and local \par \par \uc2  Mexican-American business interests about new export \par \par \uc2  opportunities in Mexico for U.S. food and agricultural products. \par \par \uc2  The ATO is bullish on the Mexican market for a number reasons, \par \par \uc2  including Mexico's increased focus on exports, better bank \par \par \uc2  regulation, improved management of the exchange rate, a growing \par \par \uc2  population of young consumers, and an expanding tourism and \par \par \uc2  restaurant sector, among others. \par \par \uc2  In particular, Mexico's tourism industry is booming-various \par \par \uc2  marketing reports and research studies show that the number of \par \par \uc2  five, four, and three star hotels has increased, on average, 24, \par \par \uc2  13, and 10 percent, respectively, every year from 1992 to 1996. \par \par \uc2  During this same period, the number of foreign visitors to Mexico \par \par \uc2  grew three percent. On average, these tourists stayed in Mexico \par \par \uc2  for 10 days, during which time they spent a lot of money: in \par \par \uc2  1997 alone, foreign visitors spent over US$7.6 billion in Mexico. \par \par \uc2  ATO/Mexico sees the growth in this sector as a prime opportunity \par \par \uc2  for increased exports of U.S. food and agricultural products, and \par \par \uc2  is actively participating in trade events, like EXPHOTEL 98, to \par \par \uc2  maximize the exposure of the U.S. food sector in Mexico. (Source: \par \par \uc2  ATO/Mexico). \par \par \uc2  QUOTA FOR CHILEAN APPLES FILLED \par \par \uc2  ------------------------------- \par \par \uc2  The Secretariat of Commerce (SECOFI) announced on May 22, 1998 \par \par \uc2  that the tariff-rate quota for Chilean apples was filled for \par \par \uc2  1998. Under the Free Trade Agreement between Mexico and Chile, \par \par \uc2  imported apples from Chile to Mexico are charged a 13.3 percent \par \par \uc2  duty for the 1998 in-quota quantity of 2,156.7 metric tons. \par \par \uc2  With the quota now filled, the duty snaps back to the normal \par \par \uc2  tariff rate of 20 percent. According to official Mexican trade \par \par \uc2  data, imports of Chilean apples totalled about 2,422 MT in 1994, \par \par \uc2  zero in 1995, 137 MT in 1996 and 43.7 MT in 1997. This year, \par \par \uc2  however, Chilean apples had a better opportunity in the Mexican \par \par \uc2  market due to the absence of most U.S. apples until the \par \par \uc2  suspension agreement reached earlier this month which ended the \par \par \uc2  101 percent duty under Mexico's antidumping action against U.S. \par \par \uc2  red and golden delicious apples. See our reports MX8046 and \par \par \uc2  MX8007 for further information. (Source: Diario Oficial 5/22/98) \par \par \uc2  FAS/MEXICO COMMENT: Since the suspension agreement, the duty for \par \par \uc2  1998 on imported U.S. apples are now 10 percent for the in-quota \par \par \uc2  safeguard volume of 61,901.97 MT and 20 percent for any imports \par \par \uc2  above that level. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CENTRAL BANK\b0 \highlight\uc2  STABILISES PESO \par \par \uc2  - \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday, \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reportedly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \par \par \uc2  market with US$10 million to help stabilise the peso, which \par \par \uc2  closed that day at US$1.00 = 8.86 pesos. The relatively sharp \par \par \uc2  decline was attributed to nervous international investors pulling \par \par \uc2  out of emerging markets like Mexico. Mexico's free-floating \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is said to often be subject to speculation by investors \par \par \uc2  who like Latin America. (Source: The News, 5/28/98) \par \par \uc2  MORE ON HFCS \par \par \uc2  - \par \par \uc2  Local newspapers reported that the National Chamber for the Sugar \par \par \uc2  and Alcohol Industries (CNIAA) claims that imported high fructose \par \par \uc2  corn syrup (HFCS) from the United States continues and that such \par \par \uc2  imports indicate a persisting dumping issue. In fact, reminds \par \par \uc2  the press, what Mexico wants is to gain greater access for sugar \par \par \uc2  exports to the U.S. above the current annual quota of 25,000 MT. \par \par \uc2  Mexico has already a sugar surplus of about 1.0 MMT and sugar \par \par \uc2  production of approximately 5.1 MMT for MY 1997/98, which is \par \par \uc2  slightly higher than previous CNIAA forecast due to good weather \par \par \uc2  conditions. Such claims are in the midst of the announcement of \par \par \uc2  the initiation of an unfair trade investigation by the U.S. \par \par \uc2  Department of Commerce on the Mexican HFCS anti-dumping \par \par \uc2  decisions. (Sources: El Nacional 5/20/98, El Financiero, \par \par \uc2  5/26/98) \par \par \uc2  TABLE EGG QUOTA AUCTION FOR 1998 \par \par \uc2  - \par \par \uc2  SECOFI has announced in the Diario Oficial an auction for quota \par \par \uc2  certificates to import 1,004 MT (1.5 million dozen) of fresh U.S. \par \par \uc2  table eggs during 1998. These imports are part of the \par \par \uc2  tariff-rate quota (TRQ) for all shell eggs established under \par \par \uc2  NAFTA. The total TRQ for all shell eggs in 1998 is 7,315.80 MT \par \par \uc2  (20.8 million dozen), including fertilised eggs. The auction \par \par \uc2  will take place on June 1, 1998 and will be conducted by SECOFI. \par \par \uc2  See our recent report MX8051 for additional information. \par \par \uc2  (Sources: USAPEEC/Mexico; Diario Oficial 4/29/98). \par \par \uc2  NUEVO LEON TO IMPROVE BORDER INFRASTRUCTURE \par \par \uc2  ------------------------------------------- \par \par \uc2  The Mexican border state of Nuevo Leon announced this week that \par \par \uc2  it will invest US$200 million to improve and expand the \par \par \uc2  infrastructure of its only inland border crossing point at \par \par \uc2  Colombia. The Colombia-Solidaridad bridge is one of three \par \par \uc2  bridges servicing Laredo, TX, the most important U.S. border \par \par \uc2  crossing point with Mexico. The Nuevo Leon government is \par \par \uc2  planning to build a highway between Monterrey and the bridge, a \par \par \uc2  cargo airport and a railroad bridge. This ambitious project will \par \par \uc2  begin with the 240-kilometre highway. A request for bids to \par \par \uc2  construct the highway will go out in June. Currently 2,500 \par \par \uc2  trucks cross at Colombia, northbound and southbound combined, but \par \par \uc2  U.S. Customs reports the bridge could easily handle twice that \par \par \uc2  much traffic. The bridge, since its opening in 1993, has been \par \par \uc2  badly underutilized, in part, because of a lack of infrastructure \par \par \uc2  on the Mexican side of the border. (Source: OAA/NL from El \par \par \uc2  Maqana, 5/26/98) \par \par \uc2  TEX-MEX RAILROAD INVESTS IN A NEW FACILITY \par \par \uc2  - \par \par \uc2  The Texas Mexican (Tex-Mex) Railway Co. has announced it will \par \par \uc2  invest US$19.5 million to increase its rail yard capacity in \par \par \uc2  Laredo, TX. The project will give Tex-Mex the largest rail yard \par \par \uc2  facility on the U.S.-Mexico border. Tex-Mex has already broken \par \par \uc2  ground and begun construction at the 408-acre site east of \par \par \uc2  Laredo. The project will help to meet the expected growth in \par \par \uc2  rail traffic over the next three years, as well as help alleviate \par \par \uc2  congestion problems within the city of Laredo. The new facility \par \par \uc2  will include office space and areas for U.S. Customs, INS and \par \par \uc2  USDA to conduct inspections. Tex-Mex is currently crossing over \par \par \uc2  9,000 railcars per month on average, up from around 2,000 \par \par \uc2  railcars per month in 1994. (Source: OAA/NL from Laredo Morning \par \par \uc2  Times, 5/22/98) \par \par \uc2  TAMAULIPAS ORANGES AFFECTED BY DRY WEATHER \par \par \uc2  - \par \par \uc2  According to the President of the Tamaulipas Citrus Producer's \par \par \uc2  Association (TCPA), the state's orange crop is feeling the \par \par \uc2  effects of the high temperatures throughout the state. The TCPA \par \par \uc2  estimated 1998 production at approximately 150,000 MT prior to \par \par \uc2  the dry spring. TCPA's President also said, local producers \par \par \uc2  cannot harvest their oranges now because there is no market, but \par \par \uc2  if they wait, they run the risk of losing a large portion of the \par \par \uc2  harvest to the dry weather. He also indicated that, like other \par \par \uc2  local producers, i.e. cattlemen, orange producers are waiting for \par \par \uc2  a government emergency support programme. If granted, it would be \par \par \uc2  used for marketing activities and improving irrigation systems. \par \par \uc2  (Source: OAA/NL from El Diario de Nuevo Laredo, 5/26/98). \par \par \uc2  SUPERMARKET'S LARGE STAKE SOLD \par \par \uc2  ------------------------------ \par \par \uc2  Grupo Gigante, Mexico's third largest retailer, said recently it \par \par \uc2  received US$ 192.4 million in cash for the sale of its fifty \par \par \uc2  percent stake in a joint venture with France's Carrefour. When \par \par \uc2  Gigante announced in February that it would sell its stake in \par \par \uc2  "Grandes Superficies de Mexico" to Carrefour, analysts predicted \par \par \uc2  that the Mexican company would receive US$120-140 million. Since \par \par \uc2  joining in the venture with Carrefour in 1993, Gigante had \par \par \uc2  reportedly invested about US$142 million in "Grandes \par \par \uc2  Superficies", which owns 13 supermarkets. (Source: The News, \par \par \uc2  5/24/98) \par \par \uc2  NEW FOOD STORE OPENS \par \par \uc2  - \par \par \uc2  H.E. Butt Grocery, seeing the Mexican market as a fast-growing \par \par \uc2  alternative to a saturated U.S. market, opened its second food \par \par \uc2  store on May 24 in the northern industrial city of Monterrey and \par \par \uc2  is said to be planning to open two more this year. The San \par \par \uc2  Antonio, Texas-based company known as H.E.B. wants to carve out a \par \par \uc2  niche in an increasingly competitive northern Mexico retail \par \par \uc2  industry by focusing on food and giving customers a taste of \par \par \uc2  U.S.-style shopping. The U.S. supermarket business is said to be \par \par \uc2  saturated. "It's a mature industry", said Charles Butt, chairman \par \par \uc2  and chief executive of the closely held company. "We see this as \par \par \uc2  an opportunity, particularly with the name recognition we have in \par \par \uc2  northern Mexico". (Source: The News, 5/24/98) \par \par \uc2  MEXICAN BREAD IN SOUTH AMERICA \par \par \uc2  - \par \par \uc2  According to local news reports, last Monday Mexico-based Bimbo, \par \par \uc2  Inc. has started bread production in its new 25 million-dollar \par \par \uc2  bakery in Peru. Bimbo, said to be the third largest bread \par \par \uc2  producer in the world, with more than 80 production plants \par \par \uc2  serving 550 million people throughout the Americas, has formed a \par \par \uc2  strategic alliance with the food company, Alicorp, to produce in \par \par \uc2  Peru. The Bimbo group reported overall sales of US$2.3 billion \par \par \uc2  in 1997. (Source: The News 5/26/98). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6h02omf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
