\par\par \b \uc2  Mexico business group opposes more peso tightening. \b0\par\par\uc2 195 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Juni 1998\par 21:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  MEXICO CITY, June 16 (Reuters) - Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should not tighten monetary policy further despite the recent slide by the peso on the back of Asia's financial woes, a top employers' group said on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "We think that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  should float, and in that sense, if it has weakened a bit now, once this international situation is over, we expect it to recover some," Gerardo Aranda, president of Mexican employers' federation Coparmex. \par \par \uc2  "We prefer a floating (exchange rate) rather than a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Aranda told reporters. \par \par \uc2  Banco de \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  adopted a restrictive monetary policy in March, leaving the money market short by 20 million pesos a day. The policy has not returned to neutral since. \par \par \uc2  Some analysts have said that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should tighten further given the recent weakness in the peso. \par \par \uc2  Through Tuesday Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has lost 10 percent on the year versus the dollar this year. The benchmark 48-hour peso ended Tuesday 3.0 centavos firmer at 8.9920/9.000 per dollar. ((Veronica Galan, Mexico City newsroom +525 728-9553, mexicocity.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6g02u25\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
