\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS - Mexico trade deficit invokes crisis deja vu. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Dan Trotta \par \uc2 696 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Januar 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  MEXICO CITY, Jan 20 (Reuters) - Mexico's surprisingly high December trade deficit of $722 million, announced late Monday, evokes unsettling memories in Mexico: rampant imports, a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a growing current account deficit. \par \par \uc2  Dare anyone bring up the specter of December 1994? \par \par \uc2  Not yet, according to economists. The country is far from the dangerous balance of payments troubles that led to Mexico's worst economic disaster since the Great Depression when a swelling trade deficit contributed to the end-1994 peso crash. \par \par \uc2  However, the alarming trade deficit for December, which was half a billion dollars higher than what was forecast in a Reuters survey of 10 financial institutions, renewed concern among economists and traders about potential fallout from a widening trade gap. \par \par \uc2  The December figure was the highest monthly trade deficit since $1.69 billion in December 1994 and it almost wiped out the trade surplus for the year, which ended at a thin $582 million -- sharply down from $6.145 billion dollars for 1996. \par \par \uc2  The government was quick to blame plummeting world prices for oil, which accounts for 11 percent of Mexico's exports. \par \par \uc2  Still, Mexican officials and investors will keep close watch on the peso, seeing whether it weakens enough to boost exports and keep rising imports at bay, economists said. \par \par \uc2  The benchmark 48-hour peso lost 3.4 centavos to 8.213/8.218 per dollar on Tuesday, while Mexican stocks fell 1.7 percent on the IPC index. \par \par \uc2  "Mexico is very aware of what it was that led to the crisis," said Felipe Garcia of IDEA in New York. "I think it's too early to say Mexico is in trouble with its current accounts. But the trend is what worries the market." \par \par \uc2  Economists already are revising their 1998 trade balance figures because of oil prices, and most are predicting a trade deficit in the range of $5 billion for 1998. \par \par \uc2  That compares with some $18.5 billion for 1994. \par \par \uc2  Surging imports and fallout from Asia's recent competitive devaluations were also to blame, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Optimists shrugged off Mexico's import growth, saying it is a sign of a vibrant recovery with inflation under control. \par \par \uc2  Moreover, Garcia noted that most of the import growth was for capital and intermediate goods, which are bought largely by exporters gearing up for more production. \par \par \uc2  The biggest difference between now and December 1994 is that the peso now trades freely. The December 1994 crisis blew wide open when the government removed controls on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , prompting its near free-fall before it later stabilized. \par \par \uc2  But if oil prices remain depressed -- and there are mixed opinions on this point -- while consumer demand for imports stays strong, the peso again will draw serious scrutiny. \par \par \uc2  The issue could be the first test for former finance minister Guillermo Ortiz, now chief of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 -watchers already are wondering whether Banco de \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  should the peso remain strong. \par \par \uc2  "That's the $29 billion question for Mr. Ortiz," said Gray Newman, chief economist for Latin America at HSBC James Capel, in a sly reference to the default-prone Tesobonos, a now-defunct dollar debt instrument in vogue at the time of the devaluation. \par \par \uc2  Newman said he's inclined to think the government won't need to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  as long as there is some healthy weakening of peso. His year-end projection is 8.8 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "We're a long way from 1994 levels," Newman said, noting that the current account deficit then was about seven percent of GDP compared to a rough 2.5 percent projection for year-end 1998. He also predicts the monthly trade deficits will come down as the year progresses. \par \par \uc2  Others are not so sure. \par \par \uc2  "The peso weakening late last year was not sufficient to halt imports," noted Ignacio Catalan, a well-known economic commentator. \par \par \uc2  "If we project the trends for imports and exports, the trade deficit could reach $7.5 billion this year and it could get serious," Catalan said. "That would be much higher than government projections." \par \par \uc2  ((--Mexico City newsroom +525 728-9507)) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1998 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du1k05bvi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
