\par\par \b \uc2  Mexico  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  lies low amid market turmoil. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Robert Kozak \par \uc2 637 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 November 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  MEXICO CITY, Nov 13 (Reuters) - Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has taken a low profile during the turmoil that has whipped Mexican financial markets recently, economists said Thursday. \par \par \uc2  In the past Banco de Mexico has sanctioned interest rate increases and other measures to buffet the Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from internal or external shocks. By contrast, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has kept to a neutral stance during the current turbulence, they said. \par \par \uc2  "I think that what the Banco de Mexico has seen is that interest rates are going in the direction they have to, so that there is no reason why they should \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," independent \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  City economist Jonathan Heath told Reuters. \par \par \uc2  "It is letting the market find its own level on interest rates and the exchange rate, and they are trying to keep to their neutral policy stance," he said. \par \par \uc2  In September, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  went so far as to announce it might leave money markets in a long position, aiming to lower interest rates and rein in some of the strength of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  As things turned out, the bank was able to sit back and watch market forces do the job for it. The peso, which defied forecasts that it would weaken on various occasions this year, was felled by global market turmoil that began in Asia. \par \par \uc2  On Oct. 21, the 48-hour spot peso opened at 7.71 per U.S. dollar, a year-high. A week later, the market had pushed it to nine pesos to the dollar, its weakest ever. The free-floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has since gained some strength to about 8.40 per dollar on the 48-hour contract. \par \par \uc2  Rates on government treasury bills, or Cetes, have also bounced around in the last month, with a rise of more than 300 basis points on one-month paper at one recent primary auction. A week later, the bellwether rate dropped almost 200 basis points. \par \par \uc2  During previous bouts of market volatility, particularly in 1996, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to leave money markets short of liquidity, a signal it wanted higher interest rates to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  J.P. Morgan economist Alfredo Thorne reads the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  inactivity in the markets as a signal it is aiming to curb increases in real interest rates by allowing the exchange rate to take the brunt of external shocks. \par \par \uc2  "The Finance Ministry as well as Banco de Mexico want to give the message that from now on they want to absorb external shocks via a greater depreciation of the real exchange rate and a smaller increase in real interest rates," he said in a call to investors late on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The recent market volatility was the first time this year the peso has come under sustained attack, and the decision of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to leave the amount of its daily liquidity objective unchanged was fundamental to its message, he said. \par \par \uc2  In March this year when the U.S. Federal Reserve lifted its key interest rate by 25 basis points, the real rate on three-month Mexican Cetes rose to 12 percent by the end of March from four percent at the end of February. \par \par \uc2  "In October what happened was just the opposite," Thorne said. "The government is giving a clear signal, and that is that external shocks are to be absorbed by a greater volatility in the exchange rate." \par \par \uc2  Banco de Mexico Director of Research Agustin Carstens said during a conference in Miami this week that the government was "comfortable" with the peso's current level. \par \par \uc2  He declined to give a preferred level for the peso but said: "When we felt there were inflationary pressures, we would tighten monetary conditions." \par \par \uc2  ((--Mexico City newsroom +525 728 9559. e-mail mexicocity.newsroom@reuters.com)) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtbd02mhd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
