\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Mexico \highlight\uc2  unlikely to  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\uc2  to help peso-official. \b0\par\par\uc2 423 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Oktober 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 MEXICO\b0 \highlight\uc2  CITY, Oct 28 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to help the bruised peso unless market volatility darkens future economic prospects, Finance Ministry chief spokesman Alejandro Valenzuela said on Monday. \par \par \uc2  "We are committed to a free-floating exchange rate. Wherever the market sets the level, it sets the level," Valenzuela told Reuters in an interview. \par \par \uc2  But he said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would step in if volatility continued unchecked and began showing up in other key variables of the economy, such as long-term interest rates and inflation. \par \par \uc2  "Right now, that has not been the case. But if volatility prevails in a sense which does not correspond to reality, yes, we will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  openly, transparently, showing clearly that what the market is showing in the short-term does not coincide with fundamentals," he said. \par \par \uc2  Mexico's peso, after months of strength, suffered its worst fall since the 1994 peso devaluation on Monday and sank to record lows amid worldwide markets turmoil. Mexico's stock market fell nearly 14 percent before trading was halted. \par \par \uc2  The country has some $25.4 billion in foreign reserves but has been wary of fighting losing battles against well-heeled \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculators. \par \par \uc2  Valenzuela defended the country's economic fundamentals and said he was not overly concerned about the latest bout of market instability, which plagued Mexico following the 1994-95 peso crisis. \par \par \uc2  "We believe the market fundamentals are okay. In Mexico, we are confident, in contrast to other years like 1994, that the economy is on very solid ground ... it's doing well. \par \par \uc2  "We suppose that once when this volatility fades away, people will look again at the Mexican market and come back, because in essence the valuation and the economy are very solid. This economy is a good buy," he said. \par \par \uc2  Valenzuela said the market jitters will likely lead to higher short-term interest rates and could have a "small" impact on inflation in a few months time. \par \par \uc2  "The most important thing on the inflation front is to continue to have a sound fiscal and monetary policy," he said. \par \par \uc2  He said Mexico was unlikely to tap markets for debt in the coming weeks, but added that the country had met its funding needs for this year. \par \par \uc2  "We are in a very solid position right now. We have no humps of short-term liabilities that we have to deal with right now," he said. \par \par \uc2  ((--David Luhnow, Mexico City newsroom +525 728-9565)) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtas03ng4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
