\par\par \b \uc2  FEATURE - Latin  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  regimes face U.S. rate rise. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Nailene Chou Wiest \par \uc2 992 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, March 13 (Reuter) - After two years of warm glow nurtured by generous foreign capital, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of four major Latin American countries may face a stress test if rising U.S. interest rates reverse the capital influx. \par \par \uc2  Economists and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts anticipate no serious disruptions in the short term, but they say the Mexican peso could trade in a wider range and the Venezuelan bolivar could head into rougher waters, if U.S. credit becomes tighter. \par \par \uc2  Argentina, Brazil, Mexico and Venezuela, the four countries that together account for two-thirds of the Latin economy, are each pursuing a different \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime with varying degress of rigidity and ties to the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  The Argentine peso is pegged at parity with the U.S. dollar. Brazil's real moves in a band that is fixed periodically. Mexico follows a managed floating regime, and Venezuela sets a reference rate with targeted depreciation. \par \par \uc2  All four regimes have worked well in controlling inflation and boosting investors' confidence, which has prompted many market watchers to hail it as the dawn of a new era for Latin America. \par \par \uc2  But some analysts argue that the disadvantages of the regimes were not showing up because the weak balance of payments is supported by the flow of investment funds. \par \par \uc2  "In the rampant euphoria of 1996 and early 1997, any exchange rate policy you can devise will probably lead to a relatively stable, strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Ernest Brown, Latin America economist at Morgan Stanley. \par \par \uc2  The question arises how a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  can climb down without wreaking havoc on the economy, he said. \par \par \uc2  MEXICAN PESO \par \par \uc2  The Mexican peso, whose devaluation sent shock waves through emerging markets in December 1994, came under instant fire when U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan testified in Congress this month. His warning about soaring equity prices was seen as preparing financial markets for a monetary tightening. But in the second leg of his address, Greenspan tempered his hawkish tone. \par \par \uc2  The peso fell from 7.74 per dollar before the first part of the testimony to a low of 8.10 in the subsequent days. But the decline sparked no panic, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "The peso was slightly overvalued based on the inflation differential between Mexico and the U.S.," said Peter Allen, director of emerging markets research at BancBoston Securities, who saw the peso's weakening as on target. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Mexico, which announced last month that it would sell $200 million of reserves if the peso falls by more than 2.0 percent in one day, did not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Robert Hodrick, Nomura Professor of International Finance at Columbia University's Graduate School of Business, said that while \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may smooth short-term volatility, it does not change the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's value. \par \par \uc2  "It is the monetary growth in conjuction with the demand for pesos and what's happening to the monetary policy in the U.S. that determines the peso value of the dollar," he said. \par \par \uc2  Jaime Valdivia, vice president of emerging market fixed income research at Morgan Stanley, said he was "encouraged" that Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "It showed that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not concerned enough to implement the brake." \par \par \uc2  Mexico's economy grew a surprisingly strong 5.1 percent in 1996 and the improving credit fundamentals provide the underpinning of the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he said. \par \par \uc2  But the peso should slide to nine per dollar by year end, assuming an annual inflation rate in Mexico at 18.1 percent, according to his forecast. \par \par \uc2  BOLIVAR MORE PRECARIOUS \par \par \uc2  Compared to Mexico, Venezuela's bolivar is in a more precarious state, Valdivia said. \par \par \uc2  If the trend of deteriorating fiscal balance, loose monetary policy and stubbornly high inflation continues, the bolivar could see more turbulence ahead, he said. \par \par \uc2  Walter Molano, director of emerging market research at SBC/Warburg, said the bolivar is at least 20 percent overvalued, but the Venezuelan government has to maintain a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to reduce inflation. \par \par \uc2  With a weak fiscal base and a shaky banking sector, the government has few alternatives to manage inflation, he said. "They have to rely on the exchange rate." \par \par \uc2  David Malpass, an economist at Bear, Stearns and Co, said Venezuela was following its neighbours' lead by keeping the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stable against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "It is a positive and dramatic departure from the past when the government printed money and had control over the exchange rate," he said. \par \par \uc2  But he refrained from predicting whether the government had the political will to pull it through. \par \par \uc2  "It is too early to tell," he said. \par \par \uc2  ARGENTINE, BRAZILIAN \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  LIKELY TO KEEP VALUE \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Argentina and Brazil are likely to keep their value in the face of rising U.S. interest rates, experts said. \par \par \uc2  The value of the Argentine peso hinges entirely on the credibility of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board, which Argentine leaders, regardless of their political stripes, are firmly committed to, they said. \par \par \uc2  Because of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Argentine short rates will rise with U.S. rates, said Morgan Stanley's Brown. "The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is safe, but asset values will be hit hardest." \par \par \uc2  In Brazil, tight monetary policy implies a strong and stable \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , experts say. \par \par \uc2  But the country's huge current account deficit makes the economy highly vulnerable to foreign capital flow, said Molano, who estimates that half of Brazil's $32 billion current account deficit is financed by foreign direct investment and the rest by portfolio investment. \par \par \uc2  Brazil has to raise interest rates more to match a rise in U.S. rates, if U.S. monetary policy changes, he said. \par \par \uc2  Molano does not expect a knock-on effect to Latin \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  if one buckles under the strain because trade integration in the region is low -- about 15 to 20 percent. \par \par \uc2  "Whatever correlation they have with each is due to their link with the U.S. dollar," he said. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt3d08u4i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
