\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Mexico peso takes long-awaited tumble. \b0\par\par\uc2  By David Luhnow \par \uc2 501 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Februar 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  MEXICO CITY, Feb 27 (Reuter) - Mexico's peso suffered its largest fall in more than a year on Thursday due to an explosive mix of low Mexican interest rates, rising long-term U.S. rates and political jitters. \par \par \uc2  The peso lost 17.6 centavos, or 2.1 percent, to 7.98 per dollar in key 48-hour contracts. It was the peso's weakest level since late October, 1996. \par \par \uc2  "Panic gripped the market. Dealers began talking about the peso hitting eight per dollar," one trader said. \par \par \uc2  Nervous foreign banks and a few locals began selling the peso in early trade due to rising U.S. long bond yields and worries about Mexico's recent drug scandals and their effect on Washington's certification of Mexico's cooperation in the war on drugs. \par \par \uc2  Speculators swooped in soon after, seizing the opportunity to sell pesos in the most skittish \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in months. Once the peso hit certain levels, programmed sell orders by banks worsened the losses. \par \par \uc2  "Speculation snowballed once foreign banks began to dump pesos on programmed stop losses," another local dealer said. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market despite having outlined a plan in recent weeks to sell dollars on days when the peso fell by more than two percent. \par \par \uc2  A Banco de \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  spokeswoman said the bank did not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  because the peso had not fallen by more than two percent before 1215 p.m. local/1815 GMT -- the time limit for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell dollars. \par \par \uc2  Most economists had been expecting the peso to fall for some months after it had slowly gained strength during the past 15 months on foreign flows into Mexican securities. \par \par \uc2  As foreigners hunted for bargains in the country's stocks and bonds following the 1995 crisis, they sparked a virtous cycle that strengthened the peso and boosted dollar returns, prompting Economists said a correction was only a matter of time, but said the peso should resist a uncontrolled fall. \par \par \uc2  "The correction is here," said I.D.E.A. in a market commentary. Local banks may unwind some of their remaining short positions on the dollar and extend the correction slightly, I.D.E.A. added. \par \par \uc2  Trouble began on Wednesday, when comments by U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan forced U.S. long-term interest rates higher at a time when Mexican interest rates had fallen to their lowest levels since before the 1994 peso devaluation. \par \par \uc2  Political worries have also mounted in recent weeks following back-to-back drug corruption scandals, including the arrest of Mexico's anti-drug czar on allegations he was linked to a powerful drug lord. \par \par \uc2  Although Washington could decertify Mexico's anti-drug efforts, a move that means sanctions, most observers believe the United States will waive decertification on national security grounds. Mexico is the U.S.'s third-largest trade partner. \par \par \uc2  ((--Mexico City newsroom +525 728-9565)) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt2s09eyp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
