\par\par \b \uc2  Mexico Cetes rate ends up for third straight day. \b0\par\par\uc2 214 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Januar 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  MEXICO CITY, Jan 10 (Reuter) - Mexican overnight Cetes rates closed up 60 basis points at 24.80 percent, their third consecutive gain, after zigzagging through a session fraught by speculation in the wake of volatile international markets, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "Although the system was supposed to be liquid, some are still witholding their cash, and the fact that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is draining liquidity helps them," a trader said. \par \par \uc2  Banco de Mexico drained 1.602 billion pesos of liquidity by offering to purchase paper through three auctions on the secondary market. \par \par \uc2  Overnight rates on bank paper meanwhile closed up 65 basis points at 25.75 percent. \par \par \uc2  Dealers noted that volatility in yields on U.S. long bonds triggered jitters and instability in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market which thus affected the money market. \par \par \uc2  Other traders said secondary rates were gaining on a technical adjustment. \par \par \uc2  "Some big banks sold paper at the beginning of the week, when secondary rates fell substantially, and now they're climbing back up," another dealer said. \par \par \uc2  No credit maturities were expected for Monday, however, 602 million pesos was expected to return to the system following Friday's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  ((--Patricia Lezama, \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  City newsroom +525 728-9554)) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt1a07xqo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
