\par\par \b \uc2  Mexico peso fights back but still ends off. \b0\par\par\uc2 546 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Oktober 1996\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1996 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  MEXICO CITY, Oct 16 (Reuter) - The Mexican peso recovered some ground lost to return to below its support at 7.80 per dollar by the close on Wednesday, but was still reeling from an early speculative onslaught, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "There were dealers who had surplus dollar positions and began to sell them when they saw the peso was recovering," a trader said, explaining the fight-back from early session lows. \par \par \uc2  The benchmark 48-hour peso and the 24-hour contract closed 5.6 and 5.9 centavos weaker, respectively, at 7.7800/7.7850 and 7.7750/7.7950 per dollar. \par \par \uc2  The same-day peso had earlier closed 6.5 centavos weaker at 7.7850/7.8000 per dollar.Traders said that foreign buyers stepping into the Mexican stock market also prompted local dealers to sell dollars. \par \par \uc2  The 48-hour peso earlier reached its weakest level this year, at 7.8250/7.8450 per dollar, as panic and speculation seized the market in early trade. \par \par \uc2  Traders said expectations that Tuesday night's substantial primary interest rate hikes might shore up the peso were dashed when traders realized that those holding dollars were not very willing to part with them in an uncertain situation. \par \par \uc2  The market was already nervous as the peso had weakened considerably in recent sessions and foreign investors had shunned the Mexican markets following a watering down in the government's petrochemical privatization plans. \par \par \uc2  Other traders pointed to comments made by U.S. economist and MIT professor Rudiger Dornbusch to the local press criticising the cental bank's policies. \par \par \uc2  "Banco de Mexico is bad news for the peso, not people who predict there will be difficulties," Dornbusch was quoted as saying on the front page of the Reforma newspaper, which many traders read. \par \par \uc2  In a Reuters interview, Dornbusch said he had not accused the Banco de \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, but of using monetary policy as a brake on peso depreciation. \par \par \uc2  "I have not said that (Banco de \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market directly). I said that if you allow me to control the (monetary) aggregates, I control the interest rates, which controls the peso," he said. \par \par \uc2  On Monday, Banco de Mexico had announced it would allow the secondary money market to close on Tuesday with a liquidity shortfall of 20 million pesos, as opposed to balancing liquidity flows exactly, as it had done since August 16. \par \par \uc2  Many traders saw this move as designed to push up interest rates and shore up the peso, which had already begun to totter. \par \par \uc2  Dornbusch said this move was valid as a temporary move, but should not aim to help the peso recoup lost ground. \par \par \uc2  "That is alright for a brief moment, just to stabilize expectations and to say we are not looking for big drop... But if they want to bring the peso back, that is lunacy," he said. \par \par \uc2  Traders said they did not spot any \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market on Wednesday, although several said they expected the Banco de Mexico to step in selling dollars if the peso nudged up to levels around 7.85 per dollar. \par \par \uc2  --Martin Roberts, Mexico City newsroom +525 728-7907. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1996 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011018dsag01pat\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picw