\par\par \b \uc2  Mexican peso could hit 8 per dollar soon - analysts. \b0\par\par\uc2  By David Luhnow \par \uc2 612 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Oktober 1996\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1996 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  MEXICO CITY, Oct 16 (Reuter) - Battered over the past week, Mexico's peso will likely fall further against the dollar in the next few weeks before recovering slightly towards the year end, economists said on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Although it had been one of the world's strongest \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year against the dollar, the peso has suddenly fallen victim to growing worries about its value and domestic seasonal factors such as inflation. \par \par \uc2  In the past week, the peso has fallen from about 7.52 per dollar to 7.80 per dollar -- a slide that has worried traders who still remember the turbulent months that followed its spectacular December 1994 crash. \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday, the peso was down 8 centavos at midday to 7.79/7.805 per dollar in its widely-watched 48-hour contracts. \par \par \uc2  Some Mexican economists were sanguine about the peso on Wednesday, saying they had expected the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to begin adjusting toward year-end expectations of about 8.00 per dollar. \par \par \uc2  A survey of Mexican economists by Reuters MXAAP forecasts a year-end peso at 7.86 per dollar -- only slightly below current levels. Analysts had originally forecast a lower level for the peso, but many adjusted their predictions in recent weeks, thinking the peso might stay strong. \par \par \uc2  "This is a temporary thing...we had a similar situation during this time last year, but our fundamentals are much better right now," said a top economist at a top Mexican bank. \par \par \uc2  The economist expects the peso to test 8.00 per dollar in the coming weeks as investors buy dollars to protect themselves against any surprises in the government's economic plan for 1997, which is due to be unveiled before November 15. \par \par \uc2  The government plan will include public sector price rises for next year that could affect inflation expectations. \par \par \uc2  "We are in a turbulent area. Until the new government plan is announced, we cannot discount new shocks," said Enrique Quintana, a columnist for \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  City daily Reforma. \par \par \uc2  Threat of interest rate or \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Banco de \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely prevent an all-out speculative attack, however. Last year, the Banco de Mexico ended a run on the peso in November by selling dollars. \par \par \uc2  Economists said the peso's current weakness can be explained also by fundamentals. Interest rates in Mexico had fallen most of this year and were their lowest in both real and nominal terms since before the devaluation. \par \par \uc2  Declining trade surpluses during August and September, meanwhile, added weight to comments by U.S. economist Rudiger Dornbusch that the peso was in danger of becoming overvalued. \par \par \uc2  "This is a natural cycle for the peso under the current system...interest rates fall until some exogenous factor causes the peso to fall and rates to back up," said Ernesto O'Farrill, director of Mexico City consultant Bursametrica. \par \par \uc2  The government's move to scale back its privatization of the petrochemicals industry also had a psychological effect on investors -- giving some an excuse to sell pesos. \par \par \uc2  O'Farrill said the peso's ups and downs will likely continue until the Banco de Mexico can build enough reserves to credibly defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Net \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  assets remain below $4 billion, according to latest official figures. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should have been more aggressive in building reserves this year. That would avoid these types of shocks," he said. \par \par \uc2  O'Farrill sees a year-end peso at 8.10 per dollar. He expects interest rates to rise again in the coming weeks as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  tries to defend the peso. \par \par \uc2  -- Mexico City newsroom +525 728 9565 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1996 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011018dsag01pah\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
