\par\par \b \uc2  Bear Stearns praises Mexico's monetary policy. \b0\par\par\uc2 310 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Juni 1996\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1996 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  MEXICO CITY, June 20 (Reuter) - Mexico's monetary policy is working well and is no longer the Achilles' heel in the country's return to economic growth following the peso crash, Bear Stearns said on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "In meetings this week with senior government officials in Mexico City, we confirmed our sense that Mexico's monetary policy is operating well and is no longer the weak link in Mexico's economic program," Bear Stearns' economist David Malpass wrote in a statement. \par \par \uc2  Malpass said Mexico is using tight monetary policy to keep the peso strong and ensure lower interest rates. \par \par \uc2  "Mexico's formal policy is still to use its net domestic credit targets to guide monetary policy while floating both interest rates and the exchange rate. In practice, however, it wants to use peso strength to assure steadily lower interest rates," Malpass said in the statement. \par \par \uc2  The latest example of this was two weeks ago, when peso instability caused interest rates to rise and the Banco de Mexico to drain liquidity from the money market, Malpass said. The peso recovered. \par \par \uc2  He said Banco de \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  has no plans to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to keep the peso strong or build reserves. \par \par \uc2  "By leaving international reserves at minimum levels, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  hopes to avoid the artificially high interest rates which occur when a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sells local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt to avoid an expansion of the money supply while it is buying dollars to accumulate reserves," Malpass wrote. \par \par \uc2  Mexico's peso-focused policy should allow interest rates to fall, occasionally rebounding to control any peso weakness. At some point, however, Mexico will have to weigh the benefits of a relatively stronger peso to inflation versus the cost to exporters and market sentiment, Malpass said. \par \par \uc2  -- David Luhnow, Mexico City newsroom 525 7289565 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1996 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011018ds6k06f8v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
