\par\par \b \uc2  Once a laughingstock, Mexican peso could be too strong. \b0\par\par\uc2  By David Luhnow \par \uc2 656 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Mai 1996\par 21:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1996 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  MEXICO CITY, May 24 (Reuter) - Once it was embarrassingly weak. Now it's uncomfortably strong. \par \par \uc2  Economists here are starting to worry about the rise of Mexico's peso, which has gained sharply against the dollar this year in real terms. \par \par \uc2  Some even want Mexico to start selling pesos for dollars on the open market to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  down and to build reserves -- something that was unthinkable last year, when the peso wobbled with every economic worry. \par \par \uc2  "Now would be as good a time as any to purchase dollars at a fairly attractive price," said Gray Newman, an economist at James Capel in \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  City. \par \par \uc2  Banco de \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the markets to keep the peso from appreciating above current levels at 7.38 per dollar, believes Denis Parisien, an analyst at consultants I.D.E.A. in New York. \par \par \uc2  After losing nearly half its value in 1995 after the bungled December 1994 devaluation, the peso has staged a quiet comeback this year amid growing confidence that Mexico is on the verge of recovery. \par \par \uc2  According to market traders, the peso has gained about 16 percent against the dollar in real terms since January, at a time when the dollar has been at its strongest level in years against other benchmark \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the German mark and Japanese yen. \par \par \uc2  "It depends on how you look at the peso, but I think it has crossed the line and has become a touch overvalued," Parisien said. \par \par \uc2  Burned by the devaluation, Mexican officials avoid making comments about desirable peso levels. \par \par \uc2  A \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official, who declined to be identified, said in an interview he was not concerned about the recent peso strength. He said it was understandable after abnormal peso weakness at the end of last year. \par \par \uc2  "Our message to the markets is very clear: that we are in a free-floating market and we intend to stick to it," said the official. \par \par \uc2  But some officials have conceded that Mexico could conceivably at some point in the future buy dollars to prevent the peso from becoming overvalued again. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Guillermo Ortiz said in an interview with Reuters Financial Television on May 9 that the peso was still competitive and he did not believe Mexico would see sustained large financial flows to strengthen the peso. \par \par \uc2  But he added, "In the unlikely case of having very strong flows that tend to produce undesirable and very long-lasting (peso) appreciation, then the Banco de Mexico could start accumulating reserves or something like that." \par \par \uc2  Ortiz's view that the peso is still competitive has been backed up by Mexico's recent trade figures, which show exports are still strong, economists said. \par \par \uc2  "Mexico is not running into the same problems it did before the devaluation," said Damian Fraser, head of the Mexico City's Caspian Research office. \par \par \uc2  But, Fraser said, Mexico should use current peso strength as a means to build reserves. "Reserves haven't increased in a significant way this year," he said. \par \par \uc2  Mexico's net international assets -- the country's reserves net of loans from the United States and International Monetary Fund to help it through the peso crisis -- have grown from $1.357 billion on Jan. 12 to just $1.582 billion as of May 17. \par \par \uc2  A stock of reserves and a slightly weaker peso now might help Mexico weather any future peso weakness, if foreign inflows to Mexico dry up. \par \par \uc2  Economists also warn about the longer-run dangers of allowing a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to become overvalued, especially in a country like Mexico with a history of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crises. \par \par \uc2  Mexico has in the past found it politically easy to have a strong peso, which makes imports cheaper and helps consumer purchasing power, economists said. \par \par \uc2  The government might again be tempted to boost employment through a strong peso ahead of 1997 mid-term elections, they added. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1996 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011018ds5o07ghw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
