\par\par \b \uc2  Mexico experts say peso strength should be curbed. \b0\par\par\uc2  By David Luhnow \par \uc2 770 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Mai 1996\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1996 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  MEXICO CITY, May 24 (Reuter) - Once it was embarrassingly weak. Now it's uncomfortably strong. \par \par \uc2  Economists here are starting to worry about the rise of Mexico's peso, which has gained sharply against the dollar this year in real terms. \par \par \uc2  Some even want Mexico to start selling pesos for dollars on the open market to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  down and to build reserves -- something which was unthinkable last year when the peso wobbled with every economic worry. \par \par \uc2  "Now would be as good a time as any to purchase dollars at a fairly attractive price," Gray Newman, an economist at James Capel in \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  City, told Reuters. \par \par \uc2  Banco de \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the markets to keep the peso from appreciating above current levels at 7.38 per dollar, believes Denis Parisien, an analyst at consultants I.D.E.A. in New York. \par \par \uc2  "If the peso continues to challenge the 7.38 level, Mexican authorities are very likely (to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ), given the need to accumulate reserves," Parisien said. \par \par \uc2  After losing nearly half its value in the year that followed the bungled December 1994 devaluation, the peso has staged a quiet comeback this year amid growing confidence that Mexico is on the verge of recovery. It closed Friday at 7.386. \par \par \uc2  According to market players, the peso has gained about 16 percent against the dollar in real terms since January, at a time when the dollar has been at its strongest level in years against other benchmark \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the mark and yen. \par \par \uc2  Most economists say the peso was overvalued by about 20-25 percent before the devaluation so peso gains have sparked a debate about whether Mexico's money is again overvalued. \par \par \uc2  "It depends on how you look at the peso, but I think it has crossed the line and has become a touch overvalued," Parisien said. \par \par \uc2  Burned by the devaluation, Mexican officials avoid making comments about desirable peso levels. \par \par \uc2  A \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official, who declined to be named, told Reuters he is not concerned about the recent peso strength. He said it was understandable after abnormal peso weakness at the end of last year. "Our message to the markets is very clear: that we are in a free-floating market and we intend to stick to it," said the official. \par \par \uc2  But some officials have conceded Mexico could conceivably buy dollars in the future to stop the peso from becoming overvalued again and stalling the export-led recovery. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Guillermo Ortiz told Reuters Financial Television on May 9 the peso was still competitive and did not believe Mexico would see sustained large financial flows strengthen the peso. \par \par \uc2  But he added: "In the unlikely case of having very strong flows that tend to produce undesirable and very long-lasting (peso) appreciation, then the Banco de Mexico could start accumulating reserves or something like that." \par \par \uc2  Economists pointed out that Mexico sold pesos during 1993 in order to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  within its trading band amid heavy foreign inflows. The trading band was abandoned in 1994. \par \par \uc2  Ortiz's view that the peso is still competitive has been backed up by Mexico's recent trade figures, which show exports are still strong, economists said. \par \par \uc2  "Mexico is not running into the same problems it did before the devaluation," said Damian Fraser, head of the Mexico City's Caspian Research office. \par \par \uc2  But Fraser said Mexico should use current peso strength as a means to build reserves. "Reserves haven't increased in a significant way this year," he said. \par \par \uc2  Mexico's net international reserves have grown from $1.357 billion on January 12 to $1.582 billion on May 17. \par \par \uc2  "A country in Mexico's position would be well advised to build a significant stock of net reserves ... through cautious, transparent peso selling," added Paulo Leme, an economist at Goldman Sachs in New York. \par \par \uc2  Higher reserves and a slightly weaker peso would help weather any future peso weakness if foreign inflows dried up. \par \par \uc2  Economists also warn about the longer-run dangers of allowing a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to become overvalued, especially in a country like Mexico with a history of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crises. \par \par \uc2  Mexico has in the past found it politically easy to have a strong peso, which makes imports cheaper and helps consumer purchasing power, economists said. The government might again be tempted to boost employment through a strong peso ahead of 1997 mid-term elections. \par \par \uc2  Economists say they are unsure what President Ernesto Zedillo will do. "I sense there is no agreement on what the policy should be," said one Mexico City economist. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1996 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011018ds5o07ghl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
