\par\par \b \uc2  Mexico's Ortiz says peso very competitive. \b0\par\par\uc2 316 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Mai 1996\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1996 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, May 9 (Reuter) - Mexican Finance Minister Guillermo Ortiz told Reuters Financial Television in Tokyo on Thursday that he was not concerned about the current situation of the peso and that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was very competitive. \par \par \uc2  Asked whether the recent rise in the peso's value was a concern for the government, Ortiz said in an interview: "I don't think that we will see a repetition of very large financial flows that will cause undesirable lasting (peso) appreciation...so I'm really not concerned. \par \par \uc2  "We're still in a situation in which the peso is very competitive," he said when asked if Mexican exporters had complained about the strong peso, which might stall the country's export boom. \par \par \uc2  Asked whether the government had set any target range for the peso, Ortiz said Mexico was not targetting any range. \par \par \uc2  "In the unlikely case of having very strong flows that tend to produce undesirable and very long-lasting (peso) appreciation, then the Banco de Mexico could start accumulating reserves or something like that," he said. "But we are comfortable in the way the Banco de Mexico is managing the foreign exchanges and the money markets." \par \par \uc2  So far this year, the peso has gained about 3.0 percent against the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday, Finance Ministry spokesman Alejandro Valenzuela said in Mexico City that Mexico wants the peso to remain competitive but stable. He also said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Banco de \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 , had not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the peso so far this year. \par \par \uc2  Asked whether the government was considering issuing more bonds, such as 10-year bonds, after placing a global $1.75 billion issue of 30-year bonds last week, Ortiz said: "I think we're looking for opportunities to strengthen our debt structure." \par \par \uc2  But he added that Mexico has no definite plans yet. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1996 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011018ds5908d8j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
