\par\par \b \uc2  Competitive, stable Mexican peso important - gov't. \b0\par\par\uc2 168 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Mai 1996\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1996 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  MEXICO CITY, May 8 (Reuter) - Mexico wants its peso to remain competitive but stable, Finance Ministry spokesman Alejandro Valenzuela told a news conference on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Valenzuela also told reporters that Mexico was committed to a floating peso regime. \par \par \uc2  "We want the peso to remain competitive," he said. "It is very important that the peso is stable." \par \par \uc2  "We will keep a floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it is important to underline that." \par \par \uc2  Valenzuela said that Banco de \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the peso so far this year. \par \par \uc2  He added that the stable peso has allowed Banco de Mexico to rebuild its foreign reserves. \par \par \uc2  On May 3, Mexico's international reserves rose to $15.894 billion from $15.642 billion on April 30. \par \par \uc2  The last official \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market was in December 1995. \par \par \uc2  So far this year, the peso has appreciated by 3.0 percent against the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  - Mexico City newsroom 525 7289559 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1996 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011018ds5808bip\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
