\par\par \b \uc2  Peso strength seen easing Mexico's interest rates. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Robert Kozak \par \uc2 616 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1996\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1996 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  MEXICO CITY, March 19 (Reuter) - Mexico's battered peso has shown a surprising resilience this year and economists say its underlying solidity could allow interest rates to gradually decline. \par \par \uc2  After a year of harrowing volatility, some improving economic fundamentals and better financial conditions are helping the peso shine this year. \par \par \uc2  "We see the peso being stable and that continuing even as interest rates decline," said Rodolfo Navarrete, economist with brokerage house Vector. \par \par \uc2  Bancomer economist Javier Maldonado said, "Basically we see the peso being reasonably stable in 1996, with interest rates dropping and inflation better than last year." \par \par \uc2  The peso is reaping the benefits of better fundamentals in Mexico after a year of harsh market shocks that followed the December 1994 devaluation. \par \par \uc2  The current account deficit has narrowed dramatically to $654 million in 1995 from $24.42 billion in 1994, diminishing the need to borrow abroad. The government has redeemed all of its outstanding dollar-denominated Tesobonos, which had risen to about $29 billion at their height. \par \par \uc2  Strength in the stock market, as investors foresee an eventual end to the recession, has helped create foreign demand for the peso. \par \par \uc2  The Banco de \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  has also been discreetly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in support of the peso through intermediaries and agencies for some time, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has refrained from overtly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the exchange market so far this year. \par \par \uc2  Also last year's inflation spiral is expected to end once an expected bubble in April is past. \par \par \uc2  April prices are expected to rise three percent, and then inflation is seen tapering off for the rest of the year. \par \par \uc2  The main weapon in steadying the peso over most of last year has been very high interest rates. But analysts now see the peso decoupling somewhat from rates and drawing more on the improved fundamentals for stability -- a trend they see continuing. \par \par \uc2  "The peso has been one of the more stable \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the world this year," one Mexico City economist noted. \par \par \uc2  Despite its good behavior so far this year, economists do expect some nominal depreciation in the peso later this year as lower interest rates cut its attractiveness. \par \par \uc2  But they add that the real appreciation in the peso against the U.S. dollar will be greater than the nominal depreciation, as price increases in Mexico outstrip those in the U.S. \par \par \uc2  The private sector sees inflation in Mexico easing to between 25 percent and 35 percent in 1996, after the stunning devaluation of the peso caused inflation to skyrocket to 51.97 percent in 1995. \par \par \uc2  But economists add the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could still be vulnerable to a series of unexpected factors: higher-than-expected inflation, turbulent politics and a too rapid decline in interest rates. \par \par \uc2  "We expect lower interest rates, and the problem is that any (large) drop in interest rates could weaken the peso," one Mexico City-based economist said. \par \par \uc2  Vector economist Navarrete forecasts the peso at 8.8 or 8.9 per dollar by the end of 1996, at the high end of forecasts made by other economists in the Mexican capital. \par \par \uc2  Interest rates are expected to slip to between 30 to 38 percent by year-end on the bellwether one-month Cetes bill. At Monday's primary auction, the one-month Cetes rate fell to a still-lofty 41.55 percent. \par \par \uc2  After the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was allowed to float on December 22, 1994, it took a fall from 3.47 to a low of 7.90/7.94 against the U.S. dollar on November 15 last year. It closed today at 7.5130/7.5175 per dollar. \par \par \uc2  -- Mexico City newsroom 525 7289559 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1996 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011019ds3j005oy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
