\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Mexico \highlight\uc2  could  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\uc2  if markets unruly - Guemez. \b0\par\par\uc2 221 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Februar 1996\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1996 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  MIAMI, Feb 23 (Reuter) -- The Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would not rule out further \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the peso if the market became unruly, said Deputy Governor of the Bank of Mexico Guillermo Guemez Garcia. \par \par \uc2  Guemez expressed satisfaction over the fact \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to shore up its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  so far this year but he did not rule out that this could happen again. \par \par \uc2  "We do not want to fight market forces," Guemez stressed, adding the Bank of Mexico would fight unruly markets, as it did in 1995, if "there is very little demand and very little supply pushing the market one way." \par \par \uc2  "The idea is that we defend orderly markets," Guemez said of speculative runs on the peso. \par \par \uc2  Guemez praised the North American Free Trade Agreement and said the United States benefitted from it. \par \par \uc2  Guemez pointed out U.S. exports to Mexico averaged a 15-percent growth rate in 1994-1995 despite the hardship Mexico faced after the December 1994 peso crisis -- a statistic that makes the case against protectionism. \par \par \uc2  "Mexico has positioned itself in foreign markets, not only in the United States, but worldwide markets. This is a foothold we are not losing," Guemez said of Mexican exports. "We expect more export growth." \par \par \uc2  -- 212-859-1666 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1996 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011019ds2o012ir\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\