\par\par \b \uc2  Mexico's Peso -2: Interest Rate Drops Boost Confidence \b0\par\par\uc2 311 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Januar 1996\par 21:37\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1996, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  The peso closed 0.3% stronger against the dollar from Wednesday's close, and is down 54% against the dollar since Dec. 20, 1994, when Mexico's financial crisis erupted. The spot dollar ended 1995 at 7.6950 pesos. \par \par \uc2  Traders and analysts say continued drops in interest rates have boosted hopes that Mexico's economy will begin to recover in 1996 after a horrendous recession last year. \par \par \uc2  Secondary money market rates traded in stable fashion Thursday, after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reported Wednesday that the interbank lending rate, or TIIP, dropped 2.8308 percentage points to 45.9715%. Bellwether 28-day Cetes rates fell 2.11 percentage points to 44.70% at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's primary Treasury bill auction late Tuesday - the third straight decline. \par \par \uc2  Even beforesthe recent rate drops, economists were calling for the economy to grow 2% to 3% this year after a 1995 GDP contraction that some estimate was as large as 7%. \par \par \uc2  In other news, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reported after markets closed Wednesday that international reserves as of Dec. 29 stood at $15.741 billion, down $315 million from the week before. It said it spent $45 million to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market last week. The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  confirmed \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in last Friday's market, when the spot dollar flirted with the 7.75-peso mark. International reserves ended 1994 at $6.148 billion. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also reported that the base monetary supply ended 1995 at 66.809 billion pesos, up from 56.935 billion pesos at the close of 1994. Mexico stayed within its target of a 17.5% increase in its monetary base, although it did not meet its target for expansion of net domestic credit due to lower-than-expected international reserves. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 04-01-96 \par \par \uc2  2037GMT \par \par \uc2  1027 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011013ds140025i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
